    "lem-tdmath-periodized-empirical-field-stationary-density": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-tdmath-periodized-empirical-field",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "For a finite configuration $\\omega\\subset\\Lambda_L$, let $\\omega^{(L)}=\\{q+Lk:q\\in\\omega,\\ k\\in\\mathbb Z^d\\}$ be its periodic continuation.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "def-tdmath-local-tame-topology",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Stationarity then makes every bounded-region count integrable, because a bounded set meets finitely many unit cells.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "thm-lebesgue-measure-of-a-box-of-every-kind",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Then $R^{\\circ}$ is open and\n$\\overline{R}$ is closed, so both are Borel and Lebesgue measurable, and **every**\nset $R$ with $R^{\\circ} \\subseteq R \\subseteq \\overline{R}$ is Lebesgue\nmeasurable with",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "thm-lebesgue-outer-measure-and-measurability-are-translation-invariant",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "**No choice principle is used.** Lebesgue outer measure is defined as an infimum\nand the Carathéodory condition is a family of equations between its values, so\nall three clauses are statements about objects that exist in ZF; countable\nchoice is needed to know that $\\lambda_n$ is a measure, not to know that it is\ntranslation invariant.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "thm-tonelli-theorem-for-sigma-finite-product-spaces",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $(X,\\mathcal A,\\mu)$ and $(Y,\\mathcal B,\\nu)$ be sigma-finite measure\nspaces, and let $f : X \\times Y \\to [0,\\infty]$ be product-measurable.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "proof-step-1-1",
          "claim": "By [F1], $R_{L,\\omega}$ is the push-forward of normalized Lebesgue probability on $\\Lambda_L$, so it is a probability measure. For a bounded measurable local $f$ and $y\\in\\mathbb R^d$,\n$$R_{L,\\omega}(f\\circ T_y)=L^{-d}\\int_{\\Lambda_L}f(T_{x+y}\\omega^{(L)})\\,dx.$$\nThe integrand is $L\\mathbb Z^d$-periodic in $x$. The translated cube $\\Lambda_L+y$ is another fundamental region for this lattice; partitioning it and $\\Lambda_L$ into their finitely many translated intersections and using translation invariance of Lebesgue measure gives equal integrals. Hence $R_{L,\\omega}(f\\circ T_y)=R_{L,\\omega}(f)$ for every $y$, so $R_{L,\\omega}$ is stationary.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F3"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "proof-step-2-1",
          "claim": "For bounded Borel $A$, Tonelli's theorem and the periodic continuation give\n$$R_{L,\\omega}(N_A)=L^{-d}\\int_{\\Lambda_L}\\sum_{q\\in\\omega}\\sum_{k\\in\\mathbb Z^d}\\mathbf1_A(q+Lk+x)\\,dx.$$\nFor each fixed $q\\in\\omega$, the sets $\\Lambda_L+Lk$ partition $\\mathbb R^d$ up to null boundaries. Translating variables in each integral therefore makes the inner sum of integrals equal to $|A|$. Summing over the $N$ representatives gives $R_{L,\\omega}(N_A)=N|A|/L^d$. Taking $A=[0,1)^d$ proves $\\rho(R_{L,\\omega})=N/L^d$ and finite intensity.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "F3",
            "step 1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "proof-step-3-1",
          "claim": "The barycenter $\\bar C_L$ is a probability mixture of the stationary laws $R_{L,\\omega}$, so it is stationary. By step 2.1 and Tonelli,\n$$\\rho(\\bar C_L)=E_{C_L}[R_{L,\\omega}(N_{[0,1)^d})]=E_{C_L}N/L^d<\\infty.$$\nThus it belongs to the finite-intensity stationary domain of $\\tau_L$.",
          "step": "3.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "step 2.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "routine_steps": [],
      "finite_checks": [],
      "boundaries": [
        {
          "case": "empty",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "For $L>0$ and a finite configuration $\\omega\\subset\\Lambda_L$ with $N(\\omega)=N$, the measure $R_{L,\\omega}$ of [[def-tdmath-periodized-empirical-field]] is a stationary probability law of finite intensity and\n$$\\rho(R_{L,\\omega})=\\frac{N}{L^d}.$$\nFor any probability law $C_L$ on finite configurations in $\\Lambda_L$ with $E_{C_L}N<\\infty$, its empirical-field barycenter $\\bar C_L$ is also a stationary probability law of finite intensity and $\\rho(\\bar C_L)=E_{C_L}N/L^d$. Boundary empty: The Statement has no empty-configuration branch; its claim concerns the displayed positive-volume, density, or conditional regime.",
          "case_scope": {
            "source_section": "Statement",
            "source_excerpt": "For $L>0$ and a finite configuration $\\omega\\subset\\Lambda_L$ with $N(\\omega)=N$, the measure $R_{L,\\omega}$ of [[def-tdmath-periodized-empirical-field]] is a stationary probability law of finite intensity and\n$$\\rho(R_{L,\\omega})=\\frac{N}{L^d}.$$\nFor any probability law $C_L$ on finite configurations in $\\Lambda_L$ with $E_{C_L}N<\\infty$, its empirical-field barycenter $\\bar C_L$ is also a stationary probability law of finite intensity and $\\rho(\\bar C_L)=E_{C_L}N/L^d$.",
            "current_raw_sha256": "b0255b6b0314d0535f7167205ade968ac260e2a5c17e6a537be1493edac4e13d",
            "scope_only_not_proof_acceptance": true
          }
        },
        {
