    "lem-tdmath-superquadratic-positive-core": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-tdmath-superstable-regular-interaction",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Define\n$$H_\\phi(\\omega)=\\sum_{\\{x,y\\}\\subset\\omega}\\phi(x-y),\\qquad H_\\phi(\\varnothing)=0.$$\nThe sum is over unordered two-point subsets, so every term is evaluated at a nonzero separation; if any term is $+\\infty$, the sum is $+\\infty$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2",
            "2.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "lem-tdmath-integrable-tail-diagonal-gap",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "For $g>2R_0$ set\n$$\\eta(g)=g^d\\psi(g)+\\int_g^\\infty r^{d-1}\\psi(r)dr.$$\nThen $\\eta(g)\\to0$ and\n$$\\sum_{m=1}^\\infty m^{d-1}\\psi(mg)\\leq 2^{d-1}g^{-d}\\eta(g).$$\nMoreover, there is a choice $g(\\ell)>0$, for all sufficiently large real $\\ell$, such that $g(\\ell)\\to\\infty$, $g(\\ell)/\\ell\\to0$, and\n$$\\left(\\frac{\\ell}{g(\\ell)}\\right)^d\\eta(g(\\ell))\\longrightarrow0.$$\nFor every fixed finite configuration and all sufficiently large periods $L$, the noncoincident periodic-image tail $\\sum_{k\\in\\mathbb Z^d,\\ |k|_\\infty\\geq K_0}|\\phi(x-y+Lk)|$ is absolutely convergent.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "proof-step-1-1",
          "claim": "Write $N_i=N_{C_i}(\\omega)$. The negative part of each pair potential is bounded both by $2B_s$ and, at large separation, by the lower-regular envelope. Set $\\Psi_j$ to a finite constant on the finitely many near-cell offsets and to the envelope evaluated at $|j|_\\infty-1$ on the remaining offsets. The shell count $|\\{j:|j|_\\infty=m\\}|\\leq c_dm^{d-1}$ and the cell-envelope conclusion of [F2] give $\\sum_j\\Psi_j<\\infty$. Since $N_iN_j\\leq(N_i^2+N_j^2)/2$, the total negative pair contribution is at most $C\\sum_iN_i^2$ for a finite $C$.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F2",
            "F1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "proof-step-1-2",
          "claim": "In case (i), choose $k_0$ with $1/k_0<r_1$. For $k\\geq k_0$, partition one unit cell into $k^d$ half-open cubes of side $1/k$, put $\\chi_k=\\chi(1/k)$, and $\\Delta\\chi_k=\\chi_k-\\chi_{k-1}\\geq0$. If the cell contains $N$ points, the identity $\\sum_jn_j^2-N=2\\sum_j\\binom{n_j}{2}$ and nonnegativity of $\\sum_j(n_j-N/k^d)^2$ give at least $\\frac12(N^2/k^d-N)$ same-subcube pairs at scale $k$. A fixed pair counted at scale $k$ has separation $s<1/k$, hence $k<1/s$; summing its nonnegative weights over all scales where it is counted is at most $\\sum_{k=k_0}^{\\lfloor1/s\\rfloor}\\Delta\\chi_k\\leq\\chi(s)$. Thus the positive core energy in the cell dominates the sum of these weighted pair counts. With $K_N=\\lfloor(N/2)^{1/d}\\rfloor$, each $k\\leq K_N$ satisfies $N^2/k^d-N\\geq N^2/(2k^d)$, so the core energy is at least $$ \\frac{N^2}{4}\\sum_{k=k_0}^{K_N}\\frac{\\Delta\\chi_k}{k^d}. $$ Summation by parts writes the coefficient as an initial constant plus $\\sum_{k=k_0}^{K_N-1}\\chi_k(k^{-d}-(k+1)^{-d})$; and $k^{-d}-(k+1)^{-d}\\geq d/(2^{d+1}k^{d+1})$. The series $\\sum_k\\chi(1/k)k^{-d-1}$ diverges: on dyadic intervals $[2^{-j-1},2^{-j}]$, monotonicity bounds the integral above by $C_d2^{-jd}\\chi(2^{-j-1})$, while the corresponding block $2^{j+1}\\leq k<2^{j+2}$ contributes at least $c_d2^{-jd}\\chi(2^{-j-1})$ to that series. The divergent core integral therefore forces the displayed coefficient to tend to infinity.",
          "step": "1.2",
          "inputs": [
            "F1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "proof-step-2-1",
          "claim": "In case (i), sum the cell core bound from step 1.2 and subtract the negative-pair bound from step 1.1. The resulting coefficient of $N_i^2$ tends to infinity with $N_i$. Above a finite threshold it dominates $2CN_i^2$; below that threshold its possible negative remainder is bounded by a constant times $N_i$. Taking $h(N)$ to be half the positive cell lower bound above the threshold and zero below it gives a nondecreasing extended-valued function with $h(N)/N^2\\to\\infty$ and $H_\\phi\\geq\\sum_i h(N_i)-bN$.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "step 1.2",
            "step 1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "proof-step-3-1",
          "claim": "In case (ii), choose $k$ with $1/k<r_h$ and partition every unit cell into $k^d$ subcubes. A finite-energy configuration has at most one point in each subcube, so $N_i\\leq k^d$ for every $i$. Define $h(N)=0$ for $N\\leq k^d$ and $h(N)=+\\infty$ for $N>k^d$. If any cell exceeds this occupancy, the configuration has a forbidden pair and $H_\\phi=+\\infty$; otherwise stability gives $H_\\phi\\geq-BN=\\sum_i h(N_i)-BN$. This proves the hard-core alternative.",
          "step": "3.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "routine_steps": [],
      "finite_checks": [],
      "boundaries": [
        {
          "case": "empty",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "Statement h(0)=0 and the empty configuration has H_0=0, making its energy estimate exact. The limit h(N)/N^2 concerns N tending to infinity, not evaluation of 0/0.",
          "case_scope": {
            "source_section": "Statement",
            "source_excerpt": "Assume the potential of [[def-tdmath-superstable-regular-interaction]] is stable and regular. In addition, assume either (i) $\\chi:(0,r_1]\\to(0,\\infty)$ is finite-valued and nonincreasing, $\\phi(x)\\geq\\chi(|x|_\\infty)$ for $0<|x|_\\infty\\leq r_1$, and $\\int_0^{r_1}\\chi(r)r^{d-1}dr=+\\infty$; or (ii) there is a hard-core radius $r_h>0$ such that $\\phi(x)=+\\infty$ whenever $|x|_\\infty<r_h$. Then there are a nonnegative nondecreasing $h:\\mathbb N_0\\to[0,+\\infty]$ with $h(0)=0$ and $h(N)/N^2\\to+\\infty$, and a constant $b<\\infty$, such that\n$$H_\\phi(\\omega)\\geq\\sum_{i\\in\\mathbb Z^d}h(N_{C_i}(\\omega))-bN(\\omega).$$\nThe hard-core alternative permits $h(N)=+\\infty$ above the finite packing capacity of a unit cell. This is stronger than ordinary superstability and is not inferred from stability or regularity alone.",
            "current_raw_sha256": "c84a5b4ca079b4391ff6aa365daf4f9d30b2edb8c75c1a7131485bf32defe827",
            "scope_only_not_proof_acceptance": true
          }
        },
        {
