    "thm-tdmath-canonical-interior-density-limit": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-tdmath-superstable-regular-interaction",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "The potential is stable if some $B\\geq0$ satisfies $H_\\phi(\\omega)\\geq-BN(\\omega)$ for every finite configuration.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2",
            "3.1",
            "4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "lem-tdmath-integrable-tail-diagonal-gap",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "For $g>2R_0$ set\n$$\\eta(g)=g^d\\psi(g)+\\int_g^\\infty r^{d-1}\\psi(r)dr.$$\nThen $\\eta(g)\\to0$ and\n$$\\sum_{m=1}^\\infty m^{d-1}\\psi(mg)\\leq 2^{d-1}g^{-d}\\eta(g).$$\nMoreover, there is a choice $g(\\ell)>0$, for all sufficiently large real $\\ell$, such that $g(\\ell)\\to\\infty$, $g(\\ell)/\\ell\\to0$, and\n$$\\left(\\frac{\\ell}{g(\\ell)}\\right)^d\\eta(g(\\ell))\\longrightarrow0.$$\nFor every fixed finite configuration and all sufficiently large periods $L$, the noncoincident periodic-image tail $\\sum_{k\\in\\mathbb Z^d,\\ |k|_\\infty\\geq K_0}|\\phi(x-y+Lk)|$ is absolutely convergent.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2",
            "2.1",
            "3.1",
            "4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "thm-lebesgue-measure-of-a-box-of-every-kind",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "In particular this covers the four one-dimensional face conventions in each\ncoordinate — the open box, the closed box $[a,b]$, the half-open box\n$B(a,b) = \\prod_{i<n}(a_i,b_i]$ of [[def-half-open-box]], and every mixture of\nthem, in any combination of coordinates — and it gives measure $0$ to all of them\nwhenever $a_i = b_i$ for some $i<n$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2",
            "2.1",
            "4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "thm-sigma-finite-product-measure-exists-is-rectangular-and-is-unique",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "1. the set function $E \\mapsto (\\mu \\times \\nu)(E)$ of\n   [[def-product-measure-on-sigma-finite-spaces]] is a measure on\n   $\\mathcal A \\otimes \\mathcal B$;\n2. for measurable rectangles,\n   $$(\\mu \\times \\nu)(A \\times B)=\\mu(A)\\nu(B),$$\n   with the nonnegative extended-product convention $0\\cdot\\infty=\\infty\\cdot0=0$;\n3. the measure $\\mu \\times \\nu$ is sigma-finite; and\n4. it is the unique measure on $\\mathcal A \\otimes \\mathcal B$ with the\n   rectangle formula.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2",
            "2.1",
            "4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "thm-tonelli-theorem-for-sigma-finite-product-spaces",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $(X,\\mathcal A,\\mu)$ and $(Y,\\mathcal B,\\nu)$ be sigma-finite measure\nspaces, and let $f : X \\times Y \\to [0,\\infty]$ be product-measurable.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2",
            "2.1",
            "4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "def-factorial-and-falling-factorial",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "**The factorial.** By the recursion theorem ([[thm-recursion]]) applied to the\nset $\\mathbb{N}\\times\\mathbb{N}$, the starting element $(0,1)$ and the function\n$f(k, v) = (\\sigma(k),\\, v \\cdot \\sigma(k))$, and by the same induction on the\nfirst coordinate as in [[def-nat-finite-sum-and-product]], there is a unique\n$F : \\mathbb{N} \\to \\mathbb{N}$ with",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "5.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "thm-natural-logarithm-laws",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "The function $\\log:(0,\\infty)\\to\\mathbb R$ is continuous and strictly increasing, is onto $\\mathbb R$, and satisfies, for $x,y>0$,\n$$\\log(xy)=\\log x+\\log y,\\qquad \\log(x/y)=\\log x-\\log y,\\qquad \\log(1/x)=-\\log x.$$\nAlso $\\log 1=0$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "5.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "proof-step-1-1",
          "claim": "Stability and [F3] give $Z(L,N;\\beta)\\leq e^{\\beta BN}L^{dN}/N!$. If $N/L^d\\to\\rho>0$, the factorial integral bounds [F4] imply $$ \\limsup L^{-d}\\log Z(L,N;\\beta)\\leq \\rho(1+\\beta B-\\log\\rho), $$ so $b(\\rho)$ and $F(\\rho)$ are bounded above on every compact positive-density interval. The vacuum term gives $b(0)=0$: $Z(L,0)=1$ is a lower bound, and the same stability estimate with $N/L^d\\to0$ gives a nonpositive upper limit.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F3",
            "F4",
            "F1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "proof-step-1-2",
          "claim": "The effective domain of $F$ has nonempty interior. Fix a core cube of side $s>0$ and a gap $g>R_0$; place one particle in each selected core of a lattice of spacing $s+g$ inside a large box. The absolute interaction from any one occupied core to all others is bounded by $C\\sum_{m\\geq1}m^{d-1}\\psi(mg)<\\infty$ by [F1]–[F2]. Restricting each particle to a subcube of volume $v_0>0$ therefore gives $Z(L,N;\\beta)\\geq v_0^N e^{-C_0N}$ for every density strictly below $(s+g)^{-d}$, after omitting unused lattice cells. Thus $F$ is finite on a nonempty interval of positive densities; by its definition as a finite-mixture envelope it is concave.",
          "step": "1.2",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "F3"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "proof-step-2-1",
          "claim": "Fix a finite list of core preparations $(s_i,n_i)$ with $0<Z(s_i,n_i;\\beta)<\\infty$. For each $i$, choose a gap $g_i=g(s_i)$ from [F2] along a sequence $s_i\\to\\infty$ so that $g_i/s_i\\to0$ and the positive interaction between distinct cores in the same lattice packing, divided by core volume, tends to zero. Set tile side $a_i=s_i+g_i$ and effective density $r_i=n_i/a_i^d$. If $M_i$ such tiles carry exactly $n_i$ particles in their central $s_i$-cubes, the same shell count as in the proof of [[thm-tdmath-decreasing-integrable-tail-free-cube-pressure]] gives cross-energy penalty per occupied volume at most $C (n_i/s_i^d)^2(s_i/g_i)^d\\eta(g_i)$, which tends to zero by [F2]. Exact factorization over disjoint core cubes follows because assigning the labeled coordinates to their cores contributes $N!/\\prod_i(n_i!)^{M_i}$, cancelling the global $N!$.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F2",
            "F3",
            "step 1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "proof-step-3-1",
          "claim": "For any finite convex combination in the definition of $F(\\rho)$, choose core preparations with densities tending to its $\\rho_i$ and rates tending to $b(\\rho_i)$. If their effective densities do not strictly bracket the target $\\rho$, add arbitrarily small weights at one attainable density below and one above $\\rho$; such densities exist because $\\rho$ is interior. Their mixture coefficients can then be perturbed by quantities tending to zero so the effective mean is exactly $\\rho$. Place the fixed core types in finitely many macroscopic slabs with these volume fractions, inserting fixed empty layers wider than $R_0$ between slabs. For fixed core sizes and gaps, the distinct-slab cross energy divided by total volume tends to zero: for interaction distances at most $R$, only $O(RL^{d-1})$ particles lie in the interface strips, while the remaining contribution is bounded by a constant times $L^d\\sum_{|j|_\\infty>R}\\Psi_j$; take $L\\to\\infty$ and then $R\\to\\infty$.",
          "step": "3.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "step 2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "proof-step-4-1",
          "claim": "First reserve an outer layer of fixed width $W$ and adjust slab fractions in the remaining volume to match the target density; the strict bracketing in step 3.1 absorbs the reserved layer's $O(WL^{d-1})$ volume without assuming its one-particle capacity replaces a bulk density. Rounding the finitely many core counts then changes particle number by at most $C_1L^{d-1}$ with $C_1$ depending on the fixed preparations, not on $W$, because only boundary tiles are incomplete. Choose $W$ large enough to contain at least $C_1L^{d-1}$ small cells separated from each other and every core by more than $R_0$. Put each missing particle in one such cell. Its interaction with the fixed preparations is bounded by the same summable tail, so the correction changes log weight by only $O(L^{d-1})$. This constructs exactly $N_L$ particles for every sequence $N_L/L^d\\to\\rho$, and gives $$ \\liminf_{L\\to\\infty}L^{-d}\\log Z(L,N_L;\\beta)\\geq\\sum_i\\theta_i\\frac{\\log Z(s_i,n_i;\\beta)-\\mathrm{cross}_i}{a_i^d}, $$ where $\\mathrm{cross}_i/a_i^d\\to0$ by step 2.1. Taking preparations approaching the selected convex combination and then the supremum gives $\\liminf\\geq F(\\rho)$.",
          "step": "4.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "F3",
            "step 3.1",
            "step 2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "proof-step-5-1",
          "claim": "By the definition of $b$, every sequence with density tending $\\rho$ has limsup rate at most $b(\\rho)\\leq F(\\rho)$. Together with step 4.1 this proves the asserted limit. A finite concave function on an open interval is continuous: fix $a<\\rho<b$ in the interval; the chord slopes from $a$ and $b$ bound the difference quotients on a smaller interval around $\\rho$, yielding a finite local Lipschitz bound. This establishes finiteness and continuity of $F$ on its effective interior while leaving excluded endpoints undecided.",
          "step": "5.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F4",
            "step 4.1",
            "step 1.1",
            "step 1.2"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "routine_steps": [],
      "finite_checks": [],
      "boundaries": [
        {
          "case": "empty",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "Statement/Example scope: Let $\\phi$ be stable and regular as in [[def-tdmath-superstable-regular-interaction]], fix $\\beta>0$, and define $Z(L,N;\\beta)$ as in that definition. Case empty: step 1.1 addresses the empty case.",
          "case_scope": {
            "source_section": "Statement",
            "source_excerpt": "Let $\\phi$ be stable and regular as in [[def-tdmath-superstable-regular-interaction]], fix $\\beta>0$, and define $Z(L,N;\\beta)$ as in that definition. For sequences $L_j\\to\\infty$ and integers $N_j\\geq0$ with $N_j/L_j^d\\to\\rho$, define\n$$b(\\rho)=\\sup\\left\\{\\limsup_{j\\to\\infty}L_j^{-d}\\log Z(L_j,N_j;\\beta):\\frac{N_j}{L_j^d}\\to\\rho\\right\\}.$$\nLet $F$ be the concave envelope of $b$ on $[0,\\infty)$, namely the supremum of $\\sum_{i=1}^m\\theta_i b(\\rho_i)$ over finite lists with $\\theta_i\\geq0$, $\\sum_i\\theta_i=1$, $\\rho_i\\geq0$, and $\\sum_i\\theta_i\\rho_i=\\rho$; the empty supremum is $-\\infty$. On the interior of $\\{\\rho:F(\\rho)>-\\infty\\}$, $F$ is finite and continuous. For every $\\rho>0$ in that interior and every such sequence $(L_j,N_j)$,\n$$\\lim_{j\\to\\infty}L_j^{-d}\\log Z(L_j,N_j;\\beta)=F(\\rho).$$\nNo claim is made at a close-packing endpoint or at any other excluded endpoint.",
            "current_raw_sha256": "4c120b3cdcd4293cca134c60d90229c6c4d081ff9a08574c6304159f7575a5cd",
            "scope_only_not_proof_acceptance": true
          }
        },
        {
          "case": "zero",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "Statement/Example scope: Let $\\phi$ be stable and regular as in [[def-tdmath-superstable-regular-interaction]], fix $\\beta>0$, and define $Z(L,N;\\beta)$ as in that definition. Case zero: step 1.1 addresses the zero case.",
          "case_scope": {
            "source_section": "Statement",
            "source_excerpt": "Let $\\phi$ be stable and regular as in [[def-tdmath-superstable-regular-interaction]], fix $\\beta>0$, and define $Z(L,N;\\beta)$ as in that definition. For sequences $L_j\\to\\infty$ and integers $N_j\\geq0$ with $N_j/L_j^d\\to\\rho$, define\n$$b(\\rho)=\\sup\\left\\{\\limsup_{j\\to\\infty}L_j^{-d}\\log Z(L_j,N_j;\\beta):\\frac{N_j}{L_j^d}\\to\\rho\\right\\}.$$\nLet $F$ be the concave envelope of $b$ on $[0,\\infty)$, namely the supremum of $\\sum_{i=1}^m\\theta_i b(\\rho_i)$ over finite lists with $\\theta_i\\geq0$, $\\sum_i\\theta_i=1$, $\\rho_i\\geq0$, and $\\sum_i\\theta_i\\rho_i=\\rho$; the empty supremum is $-\\infty$. On the interior of $\\{\\rho:F(\\rho)>-\\infty\\}$, $F$ is finite and continuous. For every $\\rho>0$ in that interior and every such sequence $(L_j,N_j)$,\n$$\\lim_{j\\to\\infty}L_j^{-d}\\log Z(L_j,N_j;\\beta)=F(\\rho).$$\nNo claim is made at a close-packing endpoint or at any other excluded endpoint.",
            "current_raw_sha256": "4c120b3cdcd4293cca134c60d90229c6c4d081ff9a08574c6304159f7575a5cd",
            "scope_only_not_proof_acceptance": true
          }
        },
