    "thm-tdmath-decreasing-integrable-tail-free-cube-pressure": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "A1",
          "source": "def-tdmath-superstable-regular-interaction",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "The potential is stable if some $B\\geq0$ satisfies $H_\\phi(\\omega)\\geq-BN(\\omega)$ for every finite configuration.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-tdmath-superstable-regular-interaction",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "It is lower regular if a nonnegative nonincreasing $\\psi:[0,\\infty)\\to[0,+\\infty]$ satisfies\n$$\\phi(x)\\geq-\\psi(|x|_\\infty),\\qquad \\int_0^\\infty\\psi(r)r^{d-1}\\,dr<\\infty.$$\nIt is regular if it is lower regular and, for some $R<\\infty$, $\\phi(x)\\leq\\psi(|x|_\\infty)$ whenever $|x|_\\infty\\geq R$.",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "lem-tdmath-integrable-tail-diagonal-gap",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "For $g>2R_0$ set\n$$\\eta(g)=g^d\\psi(g)+\\int_g^\\infty r^{d-1}\\psi(r)dr.$$\nThen $\\eta(g)\\to0$ and\n$$\\sum_{m=1}^\\infty m^{d-1}\\psi(mg)\\leq 2^{d-1}g^{-d}\\eta(g).$$\nMoreover, there is a choice $g(\\ell)>0$, for all sufficiently large real $\\ell$, such that $g(\\ell)\\to\\infty$, $g(\\ell)/\\ell\\to0$, and\n$$\\left(\\frac{\\ell}{g(\\ell)}\\right)^d\\eta(g(\\ell))\\longrightarrow0.$$\nFor every fixed finite configuration and all sufficiently large periods $L$, the noncoincident periodic-image tail $\\sum_{k\\in\\mathbb Z^d,\\ |k|_\\infty\\geq K_0}|\\phi(x-y+Lk)|$ is absolutely convergent.",
          "uses": [
            "4.1",
            "5.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "thm-lebesgue-measure-of-a-box-of-every-kind",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Then $R^{\\circ}$ is open and\n$\\overline{R}$ is closed, so both are Borel and Lebesgue measurable, and **every**\nset $R$ with $R^{\\circ} \\subseteq R \\subseteq \\overline{R}$ is Lebesgue\nmeasurable with",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "thm-sigma-finite-product-measure-exists-is-rectangular-and-is-unique",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "1. the set function $E \\mapsto (\\mu \\times \\nu)(E)$ of\n   [[def-product-measure-on-sigma-finite-spaces]] is a measure on\n   $\\mathcal A \\otimes \\mathcal B$;\n2. for measurable rectangles,\n   $$(\\mu \\times \\nu)(A \\times B)=\\mu(A)\\nu(B),$$\n   with the nonnegative extended-product convention $0\\cdot\\infty=\\infty\\cdot0=0$;\n3. the measure $\\mu \\times \\nu$ is sigma-finite; and\n4. it is the unique measure on $\\mathcal A \\otimes \\mathcal B$ with the\n   rectangle formula.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "def-real-exponential-function-and-e",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "For $x\\in\\mathbb R$, define\n$$\\exp(x):=\\sum_{n=0}^{\\infty}\\frac{x^n}{\\iota(n!)}, \\qquad e:=\\exp(1),$$\nprovided by the all-real convergence proved in [[lem-exponential-series-has-infinite-radius]].",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "thm-exponential-addition-formula",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "For all real $x,y$,\n$$\\exp(x+y)=\\exp(x)\\exp(y).$$",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "def-factorial-and-falling-factorial",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "**The factorial.** By the recursion theorem ([[thm-recursion]]) applied to the\nset $\\mathbb{N}\\times\\mathbb{N}$, the starting element $(0,1)$ and the function\n$f(k, v) = (\\sigma(k),\\, v \\cdot \\sigma(k))$, and by the same induction on the\nfirst coordinate as in [[def-nat-finite-sum-and-product]], there is a unique\n$F : \\mathbb{N} \\to \\mathbb{N}$ with",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "def-nonnegative-extended-series",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Its **partial sums** are the unique sequence $(s_n)$ in $[0,+\\infty]$ satisfying",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "thm-tonelli-theorem-for-sigma-finite-product-spaces",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $(X,\\mathcal A,\\mu)$ and $(Y,\\mathcal B,\\nu)$ be sigma-finite measure\nspaces, and let $f : X \\times Y \\to [0,\\infty]$ be product-measurable.",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "proof-step-1-1",
          "claim": "Put $q=ae^{\\beta B}$. For every $N$-point configuration, $0\\leq e^{-\\beta H_\\phi}\\leq e^{\\beta BN}$ by [A1]. Applying [F3] to the finite-volume integral gives $Z(L,N;\\beta)\\leq(qL^d)^N/N!$. Summing by [F4] yields $\\Xi_L\\leq e^{qL^d}$; the empty configuration contributes exactly $1$, so $\\Xi_L\\geq1$.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "F3",
            "F4"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "proof-step-2-1",
          "claim": "Choose $K>eq+1$ and an integer cutoff $n_\\ell$ with $K\\ell^d<n_\\ell\\leq K\\ell^d+1$. For $N>n_\\ell$, $1\\leq e^{N-K\\ell^d}$, so $$ \\sum_{N>n_\\ell}Z(\\ell,N;\\beta)\\leq e^{-K\\ell^d}\\sum_{N\\geq0}\\frac{(eq\\ell^d)^N}{N!}=e^{(eq-K)\\ell^d}\\leq e^{-\\ell^d}. $$ Because $\\Xi_\\ell\\geq1$, the truncated sum $\\Xi_\\ell^{\\rm cut}=\\sum_{N=0}^{n_\\ell}Z(\\ell,N;\\beta)$ satisfies $\\Xi_\\ell^{\\rm cut}\\geq(1-e^{-\\ell^d})\\Xi_\\ell$.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "F3",
            "F4",
            "step 1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "proof-step-3-1",
          "claim": "Fix a core side $\\ell$ and a gap $g>2R_0$. Place $M=\\lfloor L/(\\ell+g)\\rfloor^d$ disjoint core cubes of side $\\ell$ in $[0,L)^d$ on the lattice of spacing $\\ell+g$. Restrict every core to at most $n_\\ell$ particles. If two core indices differ by maximum norm $m\\geq1$, every pair between them has separation at least $mg$ in maximum norm. The number of offsets in shell $m$ is $(2m+1)^d-(2m-1)^d\\leq c_dm^{d-1}$. By [F1], the sum of positive cross-core pair energies is therefore at most $$ D_{L,\\ell,g}\\leq c_dM n_\\ell^2\\sum_{m\\geq1}m^{d-1}\\psi(mg). $$ Negative cross-core energies only increase the Boltzmann weight. Splitting the grand sum by the particle counts in these disjoint cores, Tonelli [F5] and the multinomial coefficient show that the global $N!$ cancels the assignments of labeled coordinates to cores. Thus $$ \\Xi_L\\geq e^{-\\beta D_{L,\\ell,g}}(\\Xi_\\ell^{\\rm cut})^M. $$",
          "step": "3.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F5",
            "F3",
            "step 2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "proof-step-4-1",
          "claim": "Divide the last inequality by $L^d$ and let $L\\to\\infty$ with $\\ell,g$ fixed. Since $M/L^d\\to(\\ell+g)^{-d}$, [F2] gives $$ \\liminf_{L\\to\\infty}L^{-d}\\log\\Xi_L\\geq\\left(\\frac{\\ell}{\\ell+g}\\right)^d\\left[\\ell^{-d}\\log\\Xi_\\ell+\\ell^{-d}\\log(1-e^{-\\ell^d})-C_dK_1^2\\left(\\frac{\\ell}{g}\\right)^d\\eta(g)\\right], $$ where $K_1=K+1$ bounds $n_\\ell/\\ell^d$ for $\\ell\\geq1$ and $C_d$ absorbs the fixed shell-count constant and $\\beta$.",
          "step": "4.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F2",
            "step 2.1",
            "step 3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "proof-step-5-1",
          "claim": "The numbers $p_\\ell=\\ell^{-d}\\log\\Xi_\\ell$ lie in $[0,q]$ by step 1.1, so choose $\\ell_j\\to\\infty$ with $p_{\\ell_j}\\to\\limsup_{\\ell\\to\\infty}p_\\ell$. Set $g=g(\\ell_j)$ from [F2]. Then $g/\\ell_j\\to0$, the volume fraction tends to $1$, the cutoff logarithm divided by $\\ell_j^d$ tends to zero, and the cross-core error tends to zero. Step 4.1 therefore yields $\\liminf_{L\\to\\infty}p_L\\geq\\limsup_{L\\to\\infty}p_L$. The reverse inequality is automatic for every real sequence, so the limit exists; step 1.1 makes it finite.",
          "step": "5.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F2",
            "step 1.1",
            "step 4.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "routine_steps": [],
      "finite_checks": [],
      "boundaries": [
        {
          "case": "empty",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "Statement/Example scope: Let $\\phi$ be stable and regular as in [[def-tdmath-superstable-regular-interaction]], with stability constant $B$, and fix $a,\\beta>0$. Case empty: step 1.1 addresses the empty case.",
          "case_scope": {
            "source_section": "Statement",
            "source_excerpt": "Let $\\phi$ be stable and regular as in [[def-tdmath-superstable-regular-interaction]], with stability constant $B$, and fix $a,\\beta>0$. For free-boundary cubes define $\\Xi_L(a,\\beta)=\\sum_{N\\geq0}a^NZ(L,N;\\beta)$. Then\n$$1\\leq\\Xi_L(a,\\beta)\\leq\\exp(ae^{\\beta B}L^d),\\qquad p(a,\\beta):=\\lim_{L\\to\\infty}L^{-d}\\log\\Xi_L(a,\\beta)$$\nexists and is finite. This theorem assumes only stability and the decreasing integrable two-sided tail in regularity; it does not assert canonical fixed-density convergence, periodic-boundary equality, or arbitrary-region limits.",
            "current_raw_sha256": "d5b6f500abaab7b9780f0f628568ac4bab2a96a74cce776c2b720a8b47c2f969",
            "scope_only_not_proof_acceptance": true
          }
        },
        {
