          "reason": "Let $\\phi$ be stable and regular as in [[def-tdmath-superstable-regular-interaction]], with stability constant $B$, and fix $a,\\beta>0$. Boundary one: The result is an asymptotic or general-law claim and makes no separate one-particle assertion.",
          "case_scope": {
            "source_section": "Statement",
            "source_excerpt": "Let $\\phi$ be stable and regular as in [[def-tdmath-superstable-regular-interaction]], with stability constant $B$, and fix $a,\\beta>0$. For free-boundary cubes define $\\Xi_L(a,\\beta)=\\sum_{N\\geq0}a^NZ(L,N;\\beta)$. Then\n$$1\\leq\\Xi_L(a,\\beta)\\leq\\exp(ae^{\\beta B}L^d),\\qquad p(a,\\beta):=\\lim_{L\\to\\infty}L^{-d}\\log\\Xi_L(a,\\beta)$$\nexists and is finite. This theorem assumes only stability and the decreasing integrable two-sided tail in regularity; it does not assert canonical fixed-density convergence, periodic-boundary equality, or arbitrary-region limits.",
            "current_raw_sha256": "d5b6f500abaab7b9780f0f628568ac4bab2a96a74cce776c2b720a8b47c2f969",
            "scope_only_not_proof_acceptance": true
          }
        },
