    "thm-tdmath-canonical-grand-duality": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "thm-tdmath-canonical-interior-density-limit",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "For every $\\rho>0$ in that interior and every such sequence $(L_j,N_j)$,\n$$\\lim_{j\\to\\infty}L_j^{-d}\\log Z(L_j,N_j;\\beta)=F(\\rho).$$\nNo claim is made at a close-packing endpoint or at any other excluded endpoint.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "thm-tdmath-decreasing-integrable-tail-free-cube-pressure",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "This theorem assumes only stability and the decreasing integrable two-sided tail in regularity; it does not assert canonical fixed-density convergence, periodic-boundary equality, or arbitrary-region limits.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2",
            "2.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "thm-tdmath-finite-dimensional-biconjugation",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $d\\ge1$ and let $f:\\mathbb R^d\\to\\overline{\\mathbb R}$ be closed, proper, and convex in the sense of [[def-tdmath-extended-convex-function]].",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "A1",
          "source": "def-tdmath-superstable-regular-interaction",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "The potential is stable if some $B\\geq0$ satisfies $H_\\phi(\\omega)\\geq-BN(\\omega)$ for every finite configuration.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "proof-step-1-1",
          "claim": "Fix an interior attainable density $\\rho>0$ and a sequence $N_L/L^d\\to\\rho$. The grand sum contains the nonnegative term $e^{\\alpha N_L}Z(L,N_L;\\beta)$, so $\\liminf_{L\\to\\infty}L^{-d}\\log\\Xi_L(e^\\alpha,\\beta)\\geq F_\\beta(\\rho)+\\alpha\\rho$ by [F1]. Taking the supremum over interior densities gives the lower bound. The vacuum term $Z(L,0)=1$ gives the value $0$ at density zero. Endpoint values are limits from the interior by their definition, so they do not enlarge the supremum.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "proof-step-1-2",
          "claim": "Put $q=e^{\\alpha+\\beta B}$. For each $N\\geq0$, stability and the box-volume formula give $e^{\\alpha N}Z(L,N;\\beta)\\leq(qL^d)^N/N!$. Choose $K>eq+1$. For $N>KL^d$, $1\\leq e^{N-KL^d}$, so the factorial bound gives $\\sum_{N>KL^d}e^{\\alpha N}Z(L,N;\\beta) \\leq e^{-KL^d}\\sum_{N\\geq0}\\frac{(eqL^d)^N}{N!} \\leq e^{-L^d}.$ The remaining at most $KL^d+2$ terms are bounded by their maximum times this number; its logarithm divided by $L^d$ tends to zero.",
          "step": "1.2",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "F2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "proof-step-2-1",
          "claim": "Choose maximizing retained particle numbers and a subsequence with $N_L/L^d\\to\\rho\\in[0,K]$; nested-interval bisection on the compact interval $[0,K]$ supplies the subsequence. The canonical upper rate is at most $b(\\rho)\\leq F_\\beta(\\rho)$, since the one-term mixture in the definition of $F_\\beta$ includes $b(\\rho)$. At $\\rho=0$, stability gives upper rate at most $0=F_\\beta(0)$. If $u$ is a finite upper endpoint and $b(u)>-\\infty$, mix a preparation approaching $b(u)$ with an interior preparation of density $r<u$, in slab fractions $1-\\epsilon$ and $\\epsilon$. The exact packing inequality in [F1] gives $F_\\beta((1-\\epsilon)u+\\epsilon r)\\geq(1-\\epsilon)b(u)+\\epsilon b(r).$ As $\\epsilon\\downarrow0$, the left side tends to the assigned interior endpoint limit; hence $b(u)\\leq F_\\beta(u)$. If $b(u)=-\\infty$ this bound is automatic. Outside the closed effective domain, $b(\\rho)=-\\infty$, again because a finite $b(\\rho)$ would make $F_\\beta(\\rho)\\geq b(\\rho)$. Thus the maximum retained term has limiting rate at most $\\sup_{\\rho\\geq0}(F_\\beta(\\rho)+\\alpha\\rho)$. The tail estimate in step 1.2 and the lower bound in step 1.1 prove the first equality.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "step 1.2",
            "step 1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "proof-step-3-1",
          "claim": "The extension $F_\\beta$ is proper, upper-semicontinuous and concave. It is finite and concave on its open effective interior by [F1], hence continuous there. At zero, stability and the concavity of $g(\\rho)=\\rho(1+\\beta B-\\log\\rho)$ give $F_\\beta(\\rho)\\leq g(\\rho)\\to0$. The sparse separated-cell lower bound in [F1] gives $F_\\beta(\\rho)\\geq c\\rho$ for a fixed finite $c$, so $F_\\beta(\\rho)\\to0=F_\\beta(0)$. At any finite upper endpoint use the assigned interior upper-semicontinuous limit; outside the closed effective domain set $F_\\beta=-\\infty$. Hence $G=-F_\\beta$, with $G=+\\infty$ on negative densities, is a proper closed convex extended-real function on $\\mathbb R$. Its conjugate is $G^*(\\alpha)=\\sup_{\\rho\\geq0}\\{\\alpha\\rho+F_\\beta(\\rho)\\}=P(\\alpha,\\beta)$ by steps 1.1–2.2. Applying [F3] gives $G(\\rho)=\\sup_\\alpha\\{\\alpha\\rho-P(\\alpha,\\beta)\\}$, which is the second equality. The endpoint convention is needed for closedness; it asserts no finite-volume endpoint realization.",
          "step": "3.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F3",
            "F2",
            "step 1.1",
            "step 2.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "routine_steps": [],
      "finite_checks": [],
      "boundaries": [
        {
          "case": "empty",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "Statement/Example scope: Let $F_\\beta$ be the finite concave canonical rate on the interior attainable-density interval from [[thm-tdmath-canonical-interior-density-limit]]. Case empty: step 1.1 addresses the empty case.",
          "case_scope": {
            "source_section": "Statement",
            "source_excerpt": "Let $F_\\beta$ be the finite concave canonical rate on the interior attainable-density interval from [[thm-tdmath-canonical-interior-density-limit]]. Extend it by $F_\\beta(0)=0$, by its interior upper-semicontinuous limit at any finite upper endpoint of its effective domain, and by $-\\infty$ outside the closed effective domain; retain the value $-\\infty$ if the endpoint limit is $-\\infty$. For $\\alpha\\in\\mathbb R$, define\n$$P(\\alpha,\\beta)=\\lim_{L\\to\\infty}L^{-d}\\log\\Xi_L(e^\\alpha,\\beta),$$\nwhose existence is [[thm-tdmath-decreasing-integrable-tail-free-cube-pressure]]. Then\n$$P(\\alpha,\\beta)=\\sup_{\\rho\\geq0}\\bigl(F_\\beta(\\rho)+\\alpha\\rho\\bigr),\\qquad F_\\beta(\\rho)=\\inf_{\\alpha\\in\\mathbb R}\\bigl(P(\\alpha,\\beta)-\\alpha\\rho\\bigr)$$\nfor every $\\rho$ in the closed effective domain of $F_\\beta$. These are thermodynamic limits of positional partition functions. They do not assert local-observable equivalence, a finite-volume endpoint realization, or a thin energy-shell limit.",
            "current_raw_sha256": "10280f5e544bc3f1bf93d225d7a884c6a22897f6b7b050eb9ff4bc72331778c3",
            "scope_only_not_proof_acceptance": true
          }
        },
        {
          "case": "zero",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "Statement/Example scope: Let $F_\\beta$ be the finite concave canonical rate on the interior attainable-density interval from [[thm-tdmath-canonical-interior-density-limit]]. Case zero: step 1.1 addresses the zero case.",
          "case_scope": {
