      "external_sources": {
        "L1": {
          "title": "D. Ruelle, Superstable Interactions in Classical Statistical Mechanics",
          "url": "https://www.ihes.fr/~ruelle/PUBLICATIONS/%5B28%5D.pdf",
          "source_section": "Proposition 2.6, printed pp.140–141 / PDF pp.14–15; statement and proof visually inspected.",
          "source_statement": "If U is superstable and lower regular, there exists ξ such that ρ_Λ(x_1,…,x_m)≤ξ^m for all bounded Λ, m, and x_1,…,x_m.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1"
          ]
        }
      }
    },
    "thm-tdmath-uniform-tempered-exterior-pressure": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-tdmath-superstable-regular-interaction",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "A pair potential is an even Borel function, measurable for the Borel sigma-algebra generated by open sets ([[def-borel-sigma-algebra]]), $\\phi:\\mathbb R^d\\to\\mathbb R\\cup\\{+\\infty\\}$, where even means $\\phi(-x)=\\phi(x)$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "lem-tdmath-integrable-tail-diagonal-gap",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "The tail of the cell envelope $\\psi(|j|_\\infty-1)$ over all sufficiently large integer offsets is summable.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "lem-tdmath-superquadratic-positive-core",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Then there are a nonnegative nondecreasing $h:\\mathbb N_0\\to[0,+\\infty]$ with $h(0)=0$ and $h(N)/N^2\\to+\\infty$, and a constant $b<\\infty$, such that\n$$H_\\phi(\\omega)\\geq\\sum_{i\\in\\mathbb Z^d}h(N_{C_i}(\\omega))-bN(\\omega).$$\nThe hard-core alternative permits $h(N)=+\\infty$ above the finite packing capacity of a unit cell.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1",
            "2.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "thm-tdmath-decreasing-integrable-tail-free-cube-pressure",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "For free-boundary cubes define $\\Xi_L(a,\\beta)=\\sum_{N\\geq0}a^NZ(L,N;\\beta)$.",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "thm-holder-inequality-for-integrals",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $(X,\\mathcal A,\\mu)$ be a measure space, let $p,q\\in[1,\\infty]$ be\nconjugate exponents, and let $f,g$ be measurable real-valued functions.",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "proof-step-1-1",
          "claim": "Write $T_n=\\sum_{i\\subset\\Lambda_n}N_i^2$ and $V_n=|\\Lambda_n|$. From [F3], $T_n$ is controlled by $H_n+bN_n$ and the superquadratic penalty dominates $N_i^2$ for large cell counts. For exterior cells at distance at least $k$, the lattice tail gives an energy lower error at most $\\delta_k(T_n+c_tV_n)$, where $\\delta_k\\to0$. For the finitely many near exterior cells, use $mN\\leq g(N)+g^*(m)$ with $g(N)=N\\sqrt{h(N)}$. Since $g(N)/N^2\\to\\infty$, its Legendre transform satisfies $g^*(m)/m^2\\to0$: for each $\\epsilon>0$, $g(N)\\geq N^2/(4\\epsilon)-C_\\epsilon$, so $g^*(m)\\leq\\epsilon m^2+C_\\epsilon$. The exterior square-count bound then makes the near-cell contribution at most $\\epsilon T_n+\\epsilon V_n+O(n^{d-1})$, uniformly over $\\zeta\\in\\Omega(t)$.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F3",
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "L1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "proof-step-2-1",
          "claim": "Combining near and far estimates, for each $\\epsilon>0$ and all sufficiently large $n$, the cross energy obeys $W(\\omega,\\zeta\\setminus\\Lambda_n)\\geq-\\epsilon T_n-\\epsilon V_n$, uniformly in $\\zeta$. Use $T_n\\leq A_\\epsilon(H_n+bN_n)+C_\\epsilon V_n$ from [F3], and absorb the resulting linear-in-$N_n$ term into activity. This gives\n$$\\Xi_{n,\\zeta}(\\beta,z)\\leq e^{c\\epsilon V_n}\\Xi_{n,\\mathrm{free}}(\\beta(1-c\\epsilon),ze^{c\\epsilon}),$$\nfor a fixed constant $c$ depending only on the coercivity constants.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F3",
            "step 1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "proof-step-2-2",
          "claim": "For the lower bound, restrict interior particles to a smaller cube with an empty corridor of width $k$. All exterior pairs are then in the far tail, and the same shell estimate bounds the absolute cross energy by $\\epsilon T_{n-k}+\\epsilon V_n$. The cell penalty absorbs the count term and yields\n$$\\Xi_{n,\\zeta}(\\beta,z)\\geq e^{-c\\epsilon V_n}\\Xi_{n-k,\\mathrm{free}}(\\beta(1+c\\epsilon),ze^{-c\\epsilon})$$\nuniformly over $\\zeta$. The removed corridor volume fraction vanishes for fixed $k$.",
          "step": "2.2",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "F3",
            "step 1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "proof-step-3-1",
          "claim": "Divide steps 2.1–2.2 by $V_n$ and take $n\\to\\infty$; [F4] supplies the perturbed cube pressure limits. These limits are finite convex functions of $(\\beta,\\log z)$, by Hölder’s inequality [F5], since finite-volume log partition functions are convex and pointwise limits preserve convexity. A finite convex function on an open parameter rectangle is continuous, by bounding coordinate secant slopes on a smaller rectangle. Let $\\epsilon\\downarrow0$ in both bounds. The upper and lower limits coincide with the free-cube pressure, uniformly over the entire exterior class.",
          "step": "3.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F4",
            "F5",
            "step 2.1",
            "step 2.2"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "routine_steps": [],
      "finite_checks": [],
      "boundaries": [
        {
          "case": "empty",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "Statement/Example scope: Assume a stable regular pair potential with the genuine positive-core condition of [[lem-tdmath-superquadratic-positive-core]]: a nonincreasing $\\chi(r)>0$ satisfies $\\phi(x)\\geq\\chi(|x|_\\infty)$ near zero and $\\int_0^{r_0}\\chi(r)r^{d-1}dr=\\infty$. Case empty: step 2.2 addresses the empty case.",
          "case_scope": {
            "source_section": "Statement",
            "source_excerpt": "Assume a stable regular pair potential with the genuine positive-core condition of [[lem-tdmath-superquadratic-positive-core]]: a nonincreasing $\\chi(r)>0$ satisfies $\\phi(x)\\geq\\chi(|x|_\\infty)$ near zero and $\\int_0^{r_0}\\chi(r)r^{d-1}dr=\\infty$. For fixed $z,\\beta>0$, define the exterior class\n$$\\Omega(t)=\\{\\zeta:\\sum_{i\\in\\Lambda_n\\cap\\mathbb Z^d}N_{C_i}(\\zeta)^2\\leq t(2n+1)^d\\text{ for every }n\\}.$$\nThen the free-wall grand pressure in $\\Lambda_n=[-n-\\tfrac12,n+\\tfrac12)^d$, with the exterior pair energy against any $\\zeta\\in\\Omega(t)$, converges to the free-cube pressure uniformly in $\\zeta$. This statement is conditional on the genuine positive core; it does not infer that condition from superstability alone.",
            "current_raw_sha256": "49c71126a412a879d80a35a4dbb6c6c6d119595fadb1be98725b3de97d80a157",
            "scope_only_not_proof_acceptance": true
          }
        },
        {
          "case": "zero",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "Statement/Example scope: Assume a stable regular pair potential with the genuine positive-core condition of [[lem-tdmath-superquadratic-positive-core]]: a nonincreasing $\\chi(r)>0$ satisfies $\\phi(x)\\geq\\chi(|x|_\\infty)$ near zero and $\\int_0^{r_0}\\chi(r)r^{d-1}dr=\\infty$. Case zero: The Statement explicitly qualifies the zero case.",
          "case_scope": {
            "source_section": "Statement",
            "source_excerpt": "Assume a stable regular pair potential with the genuine positive-core condition of [[lem-tdmath-superquadratic-positive-core]]: a nonincreasing $\\chi(r)>0$ satisfies $\\phi(x)\\geq\\chi(|x|_\\infty)$ near zero and $\\int_0^{r_0}\\chi(r)r^{d-1}dr=\\infty$. For fixed $z,\\beta>0$, define the exterior class\n$$\\Omega(t)=\\{\\zeta:\\sum_{i\\in\\Lambda_n\\cap\\mathbb Z^d}N_{C_i}(\\zeta)^2\\leq t(2n+1)^d\\text{ for every }n\\}.$$\nThen the free-wall grand pressure in $\\Lambda_n=[-n-\\tfrac12,n+\\tfrac12)^d$, with the exterior pair energy against any $\\zeta\\in\\Omega(t)$, converges to the free-cube pressure uniformly in $\\zeta$. This statement is conditional on the genuine positive core; it does not infer that condition from superstability alone.",
            "current_raw_sha256": "49c71126a412a879d80a35a4dbb6c6c6d119595fadb1be98725b3de97d80a157",
            "scope_only_not_proof_acceptance": true
          }
        },
        {
          "case": "one",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "Assume a stable regular pair potential with the genuine positive-core condition of [[lem-tdmath-superquadratic-positive-core]]: a nonincreasing $\\chi(r)>0$ satisfies $\\phi(x)\\geq\\chi(|x|_\\infty)$ near zero and $\\int_0^{r_0}\\chi(r)r^{d-1}dr=\\infty$. Boundary one: The result is an asymptotic or general-law claim and makes no separate one-particle assertion.",
          "case_scope": {
            "source_section": "Statement",
            "source_excerpt": "Assume a stable regular pair potential with the genuine positive-core condition of [[lem-tdmath-superquadratic-positive-core]]: a nonincreasing $\\chi(r)>0$ satisfies $\\phi(x)\\geq\\chi(|x|_\\infty)$ near zero and $\\int_0^{r_0}\\chi(r)r^{d-1}dr=\\infty$. For fixed $z,\\beta>0$, define the exterior class\n$$\\Omega(t)=\\{\\zeta:\\sum_{i\\in\\Lambda_n\\cap\\mathbb Z^d}N_{C_i}(\\zeta)^2\\leq t(2n+1)^d\\text{ for every }n\\}.$$\nThen the free-wall grand pressure in $\\Lambda_n=[-n-\\tfrac12,n+\\tfrac12)^d$, with the exterior pair energy against any $\\zeta\\in\\Omega(t)$, converges to the free-cube pressure uniformly in $\\zeta$. This statement is conditional on the genuine positive core; it does not infer that condition from superstability alone.",
            "current_raw_sha256": "49c71126a412a879d80a35a4dbb6c6c6d119595fadb1be98725b3de97d80a157",
            "scope_only_not_proof_acceptance": true
          }
        },
