    "thm-tdmath-retained-hard-zero-beta-limit": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-tdmath-superstable-regular-interaction",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "A pair potential is an even Borel function, measurable for the Borel sigma-algebra generated by open sets ([[def-borel-sigma-algebra]]), $\\phi:\\mathbb R^d\\to\\mathbb R\\cup\\{+\\infty\\}$, where even means $\\phi(-x)=\\phi(x)$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.3"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "thm-tdmath-local-tame-entropy-compactness",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Then $\\mathcal H_c$ is compact and sequentially compact in $\\tau_L$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2",
            "1.3",
            "2.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "thm-tdmath-spatial-mean-information",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $d\\geq1$, let $P\\in\\mathcal P^1_{\\rm stat}(\\Omega)$, with this class defined in [[def-tdmath-local-tame-topology]], be ergodic under the full translation group $\\mathbb R^d$, and suppose its specific relative entropy $I(P)=\\gamma$ relative to the unit-intensity Poisson law $Q$ is finite.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "lem-tdmath-relative-entropy-variational-and-chain-rules",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Then\n$$D(P_{XY}\\Vert Q_X\\otimes Q_Y)=D(P_X\\Vert Q_X)+\\int_X D(P_{Y|X=x}\\Vert Q_Y)\\,P_X(dx).$$\nMore generally, for nested standard-Borel observations $R$ and $S$ with $R$ a measurable function of $S$, and a fixed probability reference $Q_Y$ for a further variable $Y$, the conditional increments\n$$G(R):=E_P\\!\\left[D(P_{Y|R}\\Vert Q_Y)\\right],\\qquad G(S):=E_P\\!\\left[D(P_{Y|S}\\Vert Q_Y)\\right]$$\nsatisfy $0\\leq G(R)\\leq G(S)$ whenever the joint relative entropy is finite.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2",
            "1.3",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "thm-tdmath-continuum-variational-pressure",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "For free and periodic cubes define\n$$Z_{L,z,\\beta,bc}=E_{Q_{\\Lambda_L}}\\!\\left[z^{N_{\\Lambda_L}}e^{-\\beta H_{L,bc}}\\right],\\qquad bc\\in\\{\\mathrm{free},\\mathrm{per}\\}.$$\nThen the limit $p_Q(z,\\beta)=\\lim_{L\\to\\infty}L^{-d}\\log Z_{L,z,\\beta,bc}$ exists, is independent of $bc$, and\n$$p_Q(z,\\beta)=-\\min_{P\\in\\mathcal P^1_{\\rm stat}}\\{I(P)+\\beta\\Phi(P)-\\rho(P)\\log z\\}.$$\nThe minimizer set is nonempty and compact in the local-tame topology $\\tau_L$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.3",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F6",
          "source": "def-tdmath-local-tame-topology",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Equivalently, a net $(P_\\alpha)$ converges to $P$ in $\\tau_L$ exactly when $E_{P_\\alpha}f\\to E_Pf$ for every measurable local $f$ with at most linear growth in the count of a bounded support.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F7",
          "source": "thm-tdmath-poisson-reference-count-properties",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "For pairwise disjoint bounded Borel sets $A_1,\\ldots,A_r\\subseteq\\mathbb R^d$, the restrictions $\\omega|_{A_1},\\ldots,\\omega|_{A_r}$ are independent, each count $N_{A_j}$ has Poisson law with mean $\\lambda|A_j|$, and the joint law of counts on any finite disjoint family is the product of those Poisson laws.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F7",
          "source": "thm-monotone-convergence-for-the-integral",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $0\\le f_1\\le f_2\\le\\cdots$ be measurable and suppose $f_n(x)\\uparrow f(x)$\nfor every $x$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F8",
          "source": "lem-tdmath-periodic-law-local-tame-transfer",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume AC, the ultrafilter extension principle, DC, and HB as in [[thm-tdmath-local-tame-entropy-compactness]]. Let $L\\to\\infty$ and let $C_L$ be probability laws on finite configurations in $\\Lambda_L=[0,L)^d$, invariant under every translation modulo $L\\mathbb Z^d$. Write $Q_L$ for unit-intensity Poisson measure and suppose\n$$c_L=L^{-d}D(C_L\\Vert Q_L),\\qquad \\sup_L c_L<\\infty.$$\nLet $\\widetilde C_L$ be the law of the periodic continuation. Then $\\widetilde C_L=E_{C_L}R_{L,\\omega}$; these laws are stationary and are relatively sequentially compact in $\\tau_L$ as $L\\to\\infty$. Their local counts are uniformly integrable. Along any convergent subsequence with limit $P$,\n$$I(P)\\leq\\liminf_L c_L.$$\nIf the empirical fields $R_{L,\\omega}$ converge in probability in $\\tau_L$ to a fixed $P_*$, then $\\widetilde C_L\\to P_*$ in $\\tau_L$, including expectations of every measurable local $f$ with $|f|\\leq b(1+N_A)$ for bounded $A$. This concerns periodic continuation, not the zero extension of a configuration outside its fundamental cube.",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "proof-step-1-1",
          "claim": "Regularity bounds $\\phi$ above outside a bounded set, so $B$ is bounded. Define $\\chi=+\\infty$ on $B$ and $0$ elsewhere. Then $\\chi$ is even, stable with constant $0$, and has zero far tail. Its finite-volume beta-zero partition is exactly the specified hard-admissibility partition.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "proof-step-1-2",
          "claim": "For each bounded region $A$, the event that $A$ contains a forbidden pair is a bounded measurable local event. Its probability is continuous under $\\tau_L$ by [F6]. Exhausting space by countably many bounded cubes shows that $\\mathcal K_B$ is $\\tau_L$-closed. Apply [F4] to the bounded tests $f_M=a\\min(N_C,M)$ and let $M\\to\\infty$. Monotone convergence and [F7] give $I(P)\\geq a\\rho(P)-(e^a-1)$, hence $\\rho(P)\\leq(I(P)+e^a-1)/a$. Choosing $a>2\\max(\\log z,0)$ makes $I(P)-\\rho(P)\\log z$ coercive in $I(P)$. By [F2], every finite sublevel is compact and lower semicontinuous, so the infimum on $\\mathcal K_B$ is attained whenever the admissible class is nonempty. The empty configuration law gives a finite candidate.",
          "step": "1.2",
          "inputs": [
            "F6",
            "F4",
            "F7",
            "F2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "proof-step-1-3",
          "claim": "Fix $P\\in\\mathcal K_B$ with $I(P)<\\infty$. Condition its restriction to a cube of side $n$ on configurations whose distinct points have separation at least $1/q$ and whose finite pair energies are at most $q$. As $q\\to\\infty$, these events increase to probability one because configurations are locally finite, simple, and hard-admissible. Choose $q=q(n)$ so the conditioning probability tends to one. Tile space by these conditioned blocks separated by an empty corridor wider than the range of $B$ and the fixed tail cutoff, take independent copies, then average the lattice phase uniformly. No cross-block pair is forbidden. The conditional entropy inequality $$ D(P(\\cdot\\mid E)\\Vert Q)\\leq [D(P\\Vert Q)+1]/P(E)-\\log P(E) $$ follows by expanding the conditional density $1_E(dP/dQ)/P(E)$ and using $-u\\log u\\leq1/e$. Additivity on independent blocks and the vanishing corridor fraction give $\\limsup I(P_n)\\leq I(P)$; local count truncation and finite first moments give $P_n\\to P$ in $\\tau_L$. Each $P_n$ has finite original energy density.",
          "step": "1.3",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "F4",
            "F5"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "proof-step-2-1",
          "claim": "For a finite-volume beta-zero Gibbs law $G_L^b$, direct integration of its density gives $$ -\\log Z_{Q,L}=D(G_L^b\\Vert Q_L)-(\\log z)E_{G_L^b}N_L. $$ Stationarize independent blocks and average their phase. The entropy chain rule bounds the resulting specific entropy by the displayed finite-volume entropy per volume; hard admissibility passes to every local limit. Compactness [F2], lower semicontinuity, and the entropy identity therefore give the pressure upper bound by the negative minimum in the statement. For the reverse bound, use the approximating finite-energy laws from step 1.3. Their independent separated blocks are admissible; the spatial likelihood theorem [F3] supplies typical blocks with likelihood cost $I(P_n)$, while empirical counts converge to $\\rho(P_n)$. Changing from Poisson measure to the beta-zero weight gives the lower rate $-I(P_n)+\\rho(P_n)\\log z$. Let $n\\to\\infty$ and optimize over $P$. Corridors change the normalized log weight by their vanishing volume fraction, so free and periodic rates agree.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F2",
            "step 1.3",
            "F3",
            "F5"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "proof-step-3-1",
          "claim": "Put $\\alpha=\\log z$, $p_L=L^{-d}\\log Z_{Q,L}$, and $d_L=L^{-d}D(G_L^{\\rm per}\\Vert Q_L)$. The exact identity in step 2.1 reads $d_L=\\alpha\\rho_L-p_L$. For $a>\\max(\\alpha,0)$, the entropy count inequality gives $d_L\\geq a\\rho_L-(e^a-1)$; the convergent pressure rates therefore bound both $\\rho_L$ and $d_L$. The grand density and periodic hard-admissibility condition are invariant under translations modulo the box, so [F8] gives relatively compact continued laws. Every limit $P$ lies in $\\mathcal K_B$ by the bounded local forbidden-pair tests from step 1.2. Along a subsequence realizing the entropy liminf, [F8] and count-tail uniformity give\n$$I(P)-\\alpha\\rho(P)\\leq\\liminf(d_L-\\alpha\\rho_L)=-\\lim p_L=\\min_{\\mathcal K_B}(I-\\alpha\\rho).$$\nThe opposite inequality is the definition of the minimum. Thus every accumulation point is a minimizer. If there is just one, relative compactness forces convergence of the entire sequence in $\\tau_L$, including every stated tame test.",
          "step": "3.1",
          "inputs": [
            "step 2.1",
            "F8",
            "step 1.2",
            "F2",
            "F4",
            "F6",
            "F7"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "routine_steps": [],
      "finite_checks": [],
      "boundaries": [
        {
          "case": "empty",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "Statement/Example scope: Let $\\phi$ be superstable and regular as in [[def-tdmath-superstable-regular-interaction]], and let $B=\\{x:\\phi(x)=+\\infty\\}$. Case empty: step 1.2 addresses the empty case.",
          "case_scope": {
            "source_section": "Statement",
            "source_excerpt": "Let $\\phi$ be superstable and regular as in [[def-tdmath-superstable-regular-interaction]], and let $B=\\{x:\\phi(x)=+\\infty\\}$. For a finite configuration $\\omega$, call it admissible when no pair displacement belongs to $B$; for periodic boxes require this also for every periodic image pair. Define the zero-inverse-temperature-parameter continuation (the mathematical $\\beta=0$ branch) to be $z^{N(\\omega)}\\mathbf 1_{\\rm admissible}(\\omega)$, explicitly retaining the hard exclusion. Then the normalized Poisson free and periodic partition rates exist and equal\n$$-\\min_{P\\in\\mathcal K_B}\\{I(P)-\\rho(P)\\log z\\},$$\nwhere $\\mathcal K_B$ is the set of stationary laws with no forbidden pair almost surely. The minimizer set is nonempty and compact in $\\tau_L$. The periodically continued grand laws are relatively compact in $\\tau_L$, all their accumulation points are minimizers, and if this minimizer is unique they converge to it for every measurable local observable of at-most-linear count growth. If $B=\\varnothing$, this is the Poisson activity-$z$ model. For $B\\ne\\varnothing$, it is not an unrestricted Poisson law and does not assign a value to the undefined product $0\\cdot(+\\infty)$.",
            "current_raw_sha256": "204387b5db66bca9abb976d3c38eddac3db8040ff40067eaa9c42ec50777ae33",
            "scope_only_not_proof_acceptance": true
          }
        },
