    "thm-tdmath-interval-event-ensemble-limit": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "thm-tdmath-continuum-variational-pressure",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "For free and periodic cubes define\n$$Z_{L,z,\\beta,bc}=E_{Q_{\\Lambda_L}}\\!\\left[z^{N_{\\Lambda_L}}e^{-\\beta H_{L,bc}}\\right],\\qquad bc\\in\\{\\mathrm{free},\\mathrm{per}\\}.$$\nThen the limit $p_Q(z,\\beta)=\\lim_{L\\to\\infty}L^{-d}\\log Z_{L,z,\\beta,bc}$ exists, is independent of $bc$, and\n$$p_Q(z,\\beta)=-\\min_{P\\in\\mathcal P^1_{\\rm stat}}\\{I(P)+\\beta\\Phi(P)-\\rho(P)\\log z\\}.$$\nThe minimizer set is nonempty and compact in the local-tame topology $\\tau_L$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1",
            "3.1",
            "4.1",
            "5.1",
            "6.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "thm-tdmath-local-tame-entropy-compactness",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let\n$$\\mathcal H_c:=\\{P\\in\\mathcal P^1_{\\rm stat}(\\Omega): I(P)\\leq c\\},\\qquad c\\in[0,\\infty),$$\nwhere $I$ and the local-tame topology $\\tau_L$ are defined by [[thm-tdmath-specific-entropy-density]] and [[def-tdmath-local-tame-topology]].",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1",
            "3.1",
            "5.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "lem-tdmath-closed-convex-separation",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $n\\ge1$, let $E\\subseteq\\mathbb R^n$ be nonempty, closed, and convex, and let $z\\notin E$.",
          "uses": [
            "4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "thm-tdmath-specific-entropy-density",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "This is an information functional relative to the specified unit-intensity Poisson law; changing the reference changes its value.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "lem-tdmath-relative-entropy-variational-and-chain-rules",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Then\n$$D(P_{XY}\\Vert Q_X\\otimes Q_Y)=D(P_X\\Vert Q_X)+\\int_X D(P_{Y|X=x}\\Vert Q_Y)\\,P_X(dx).$$\nMore generally, for nested standard-Borel observations $R$ and $S$ with $R$ a measurable function of $S$, and a fixed probability reference $Q_Y$ for a further variable $Y$, the conditional increments\n$$G(R):=E_P\\!\\left[D(P_{Y|R}\\Vert Q_Y)\\right],\\qquad G(S):=E_P\\!\\left[D(P_{Y|S}\\Vert Q_Y)\\right]$$\nsatisfy $0\\leq G(R)\\leq G(S)$ whenever the joint relative entropy is finite.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "3.1",
            "5.1",
            "6.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F6",
          "source": "def-tdmath-local-tame-topology",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Equivalently, a net $(P_\\alpha)$ converges to $P$ in $\\tau_L$ exactly when $E_{P_\\alpha}f\\to E_Pf$ for every measurable local $f$ with at most linear growth in the count of a bounded support.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "3.1",
            "5.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F7",
          "source": "def-tdmath-periodized-empirical-field",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "For a probability law $C_L$ on finite configurations in $\\Lambda_L$, define its **empirical-field barycenter** $\\bar C_L$ by\n$$\\bar C_L(f):=\\int C_L(d\\omega)\\,R_{L,\\omega}(f)=\\frac1{L^d}\\int_{\\Lambda_L}E_{C_L}[f(T_x\\omega^{(L)})]\\,dx$$\nfor local tame $f$ from [[def-tdmath-local-tame-topology]].",
          "uses": [
            "5.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F7",
          "source": "lem-tdmath-periodized-empirical-field-stationary-density",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "For $L>0$ and a finite configuration $\\omega\\subset\\Lambda_L$ with $N(\\omega)=N$, the measure $R_{L,\\omega}$ of [[def-tdmath-periodized-empirical-field]] is a stationary probability law of finite intensity and\n$$\\rho(R_{L,\\omega})=\\frac{N}{L^d}.$$\nFor any probability law $C_L$ on finite configurations in $\\Lambda_L$ with $E_{C_L}N<\\infty$, its empirical-field barycenter $\\bar C_L$ is also a stationary probability law of finite intensity and $\\rho(\\bar C_L)=E_{C_L}N/L^d$.",
          "uses": [
            "5.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F8",
          "source": "lem-tdmath-periodic-law-local-tame-transfer",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume AC, the ultrafilter extension principle, DC, and HB as in [[thm-tdmath-local-tame-entropy-compactness]]. Let $L\\to\\infty$ and let $C_L$ be probability laws on finite configurations in $\\Lambda_L=[0,L)^d$, invariant under every translation modulo $L\\mathbb Z^d$. Write $Q_L$ for unit-intensity Poisson measure and suppose\n$$c_L=L^{-d}D(C_L\\Vert Q_L),\\qquad \\sup_L c_L<\\infty.$$\nLet $\\widetilde C_L$ be the law of the periodic continuation. Then $\\widetilde C_L=E_{C_L}R_{L,\\omega}$; these laws are stationary and are relatively sequentially compact in $\\tau_L$ as $L\\to\\infty$. Their local counts are uniformly integrable. Along any convergent subsequence with limit $P$,\n$$I(P)\\leq\\liminf_L c_L.$$\nIf the empirical fields $R_{L,\\omega}$ converge in probability in $\\tau_L$ to a fixed $P_*$, then $\\widetilde C_L\\to P_*$ in $\\tau_L$, including expectations of every measurable local $f$ with $|f|\\leq b(1+N_A)$ for bounded $A$. This concerns periodic continuation, not the zero extension of a configuration outside its fundamental cube.",
          "uses": [
            "6.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F9",
          "source": "thm-tdmath-retained-hard-zero-beta-limit",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $\\phi$ be superstable and regular as in [[def-tdmath-superstable-regular-interaction]], and let $B=\\{x:\\phi(x)=+\\infty\\}$. For a finite configuration $\\omega$, call it admissible when no pair displacement belongs to $B$; for periodic boxes require this also for every periodic image pair. Define the zero-inverse-temperature-parameter continuation (the mathematical $\\beta=0$ branch) to be $z^{N(\\omega)}\\mathbf 1_{\\rm admissible}(\\omega)$, explicitly retaining the hard exclusion. Then the normalized Poisson free and periodic partition rates exist and equal\n$$-\\min_{P\\in\\mathcal K_B}\\{I(P)-\\rho(P)\\log z\\},$$\nwhere $\\mathcal K_B$ is the set of stationary laws with no forbidden pair almost surely. The minimizer set is nonempty and compact in $\\tau_L$. The periodically continued grand laws are relatively compact in $\\tau_L$, all their accumulation points are minimizers, and if this minimizer is unique they converge to it for every measurable local observable of at-most-linear count growth. If $B=\\varnothing$, this is the Poisson activity-$z$ model. For $B\\ne\\varnothing$, it is not an unrestricted Poisson law and does not assign a value to the undefined product $0\\cdot(+\\infty)$.",
          "uses": [
            "4.1",
            "5.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "proof-step-1-1",
          "claim": "Define $m(\\rho,e)=\\inf\\{I(P):\\rho(P)=\\rho,\\Phi(P)\\leq e\\}$ and $s=-m$. For stationary finite-entropy laws $P,R$, the binary-mixture chain rule on a bounded cube $G$ gives $\\theta D(P_G\\Vert Q_G)+(1-\\theta)D(R_G\\Vert Q_G)=D((\\theta P+(1-\\theta)R)_G\\Vert Q_G)+I(S;X_G)$, with $0\\leq I(S;X_G)\\leq H(S)<\\infty$. Divide by $|G|$ and use [F4]; the label entropy vanishes, so $I$ is affine on finite mixtures. The expectation $\\rho$ and finite-volume energy expectations are affine, hence their limits $\\rho,\\Phi$ are affine where finite. Mixtures of feasible laws show $m$ is convex and $s$ concave. If $(\\rho_n,e_n)\\to(\\rho,e)$ and $\\liminf m(\\rho_n,e_n)<\\infty$, choose near-minimizers with bounded $I$. Compactness [F2] gives a convergent subsequence; continuity of $\\rho$ by [F6] and lower semicontinuity of $\\Phi,I$ in [F1] show the limit is feasible with entropy no greater than the liminf. Thus $m$ is lower semicontinuous and $s$ upper semicontinuous; the same compactness argument attains every finite infimum. For strict feasibility, choose $\\rho_1$ in the interior of $D$ below $\\nu(\\phi)$ with a finite-energy, finite-entropy law $R$. The inequality $e>\\inf_{\\rho\\in D}e_0(\\rho)$ gives $\\rho_2\\in D$ and a finite-entropy law $P$ with $\\Phi(P)<e$. If $\\rho_2$ is at an endpoint of $D$, mix $P$ with a sufficiently small positive amount of $R$; affinity of $\\rho,\\Phi,I$ keeps the energy below $e$ and moves the density into the interior of $D$.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F4",
            "F2",
            "F6",
            "F1",
            "F5"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "proof-step-2-1",
          "claim": "For the lower event bound, take a feasible stationary law with strict density and energy inequalities. Approximate it by separated independent blocks: condition each block on a finite-energy, bounded-count event of probability tending to one, separate blocks by a fixed empty corridor, and average the phase. Its entropy per volume tends to $I(P)$; its empirical density and energy converge to $\\rho(P)$ and $\\Phi(P)$. The spatial likelihood theorem gives Poisson probability at least $\\exp[-L^d(I(P)+o(1))]$ for the joint typical event, which lies in $A_L$. If a minimizing law lies on a constraint boundary, mix it with the strict feasible law. The mixture remains feasible for sufficiently small mixing weight and its entropy tends to the boundary law's entropy by the entropy affinity established in step 1.1 and lower semicontinuity. Thus the open strict event and the closed event have the same entropy infimum.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "step 1.1",
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "F4"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "proof-step-3-1",
          "claim": "For the upper event bound, let $G_L=Q_{\\Lambda_L}(\\cdot\\mid A_L)$. Its exact entropy is $D(G_L\\Vert Q_{\\Lambda_L})=-\\log Q_{\\Lambda_L}(A_L)$. Form the auxiliary stationary law by placing independent copies of $G_L$ in disjoint blocks, leaving a fixed empty corridor, and averaging the block phase. The independent-increment Poisson entropy chain rule bounds this auxiliary law's specific entropy by the displayed finite-volume entropy per volume. Its local limits preserve the closed density and energy constraints by lower semicontinuity of $I$ and $\\Phi$, giving the rate upper bound by $m$. The matching lower bound follows from step 2.1. This auxiliary block law is used only for the event exponent.",
          "step": "3.1",
          "inputs": [
            "step 2.1",
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "F5",
            "F6",
            "L1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "proof-step-4-1",
          "claim": "The tangent-plane argument in [L1] separates the strict-feasibility constraint set in finite dimension [F3]. The objective coefficient is nonzero by strict feasibility, so it can be normalized to one; this does not make the energy coefficient strictly positive. Since $s$ is nondecreasing in the energy allowance, its tangent energy coefficient is $\\beta\\geq0$, with finite $\\alpha=\\log z$. Every constrained entropy minimizer then minimizes $I+\\beta\\Phi-\\alpha\\rho$ at positive beta. At zero beta the finite-energy feasible laws are hard-admissible; the retained-admissibility extension and separated-block approximation identify the corresponding minimum on $\\mathcal K_B$. The finite-volume hard indicator is retained throughout. Theorem 4 and Proposition 7.3 give the conditional-law accumulation conclusion. Proposition 7.4 proves the positive-beta grand conclusion; the zero-beta grand conclusion follows from the retained-admissibility entropy proof in [F9], rather than an extension of that proposition's strictly positive-beta hypothesis.",
          "step": "4.1",
          "inputs": [
            "L1",
            "F3",
            "F9",
            "F1",
            "step 1.1",
            "step 3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "proof-step-5-1",
          "claim": "For completeness, the empirical concentration is the extension discussed after Theorem 4, not an additional assertion of its printed formula. The Poisson empirical-field upper bound used in [L1], Lemma 7.2 equation (7.4), applies to every closed subset of the constraint set. Outside an open neighborhood of the constrained entropy minimizers its entropy infimum is strictly above the constrained minimum: otherwise compact entropy levels yield a minimizing limit outside that neighborhood. Divide this exponentially smaller numerator by the event probability from steps 2.1–3.1. Thus the conditional empirical field concentrates on the constrained minimizers, hence on their containing grand minimizer set. For positive beta, the grand empirical-field conclusion is the good large-deviation principle of [L1], Theorem 2. For zero beta, tilt Poisson measure by $z^N$ and restrict to the closed hard-admissible class. Its upper bound has rate $I-\\alpha\\rho$; the separated-block lower bound gives its partition minimum. Compact sublevels follow from $I\\geq a\\rho-(e^a-1)$ with $a>\\max(\\alpha,0)$, so the same strict-gap argument gives grand concentration. A singleton grand minimizer therefore gives empirical-field convergence in probability in both branches.",
          "step": "5.1",
          "inputs": [
            "L1",
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "F5",
            "F6",
            "F7",
            "F9",
            "step 2.1",
            "step 3.1",
            "step 4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "proof-step-6-1",
          "claim": "The conditional law is torus-invariant and its entropy density is $-L^{-d}\\log Q_L(A_L)$, which is bounded by the event limit. The matching grand law is torus-invariant as well. At positive beta its entropy density is bounded by the pressure identity, the superstable lower energy bound, and the Poisson entropy count inequality; at zero beta use the same identity with the hard indicator and omit the energy term. Consequently [F8] applies. Its transfer result converts step 5.1 into convergence of the periodically continued laws for every local tame observable under uniqueness. No zero extension outside the fundamental cube is used.",
          "step": "6.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F8",
            "step 5.1",
            "F1",
            "F5",
            "step 3.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "routine_steps": [],
      "finite_checks": [],
      "boundaries": [
        {
          "case": "empty",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "Statement/Example scope: Assume the superstable regular model and Poisson conventions of [[thm-tdmath-continuum-variational-pressure]]. Case empty: step 2.1 addresses the empty case.",
          "case_scope": {
            "source_section": "Statement",
            "source_excerpt": "Assume the superstable regular model and Poisson conventions of [[thm-tdmath-continuum-variational-pressure]]. Let $D\\subset[0,\\infty)$ be an interval of positive length, let $\\nu$ be the supremum of finite-entropy, finite-energy attainable densities, and set $e_0(\\rho)=\\inf\\{\\Phi(P):\\rho(P)=\\rho,\\ I(P)<\\infty\\}$. If $\\inf D<\\nu$ and $e>\\inf_{\\rho\\in D}e_0(\\rho)$, define\n$$A_L=\\{N_{\\Lambda_L}/L^d\\in D,\\ H_{L,\\mathrm{per}}/L^d\\leq e\\}.$$\nThen $A_L$ has positive Poisson probability for all sufficiently large $L$, and\n$$\\lim_{L\\to\\infty}L^{-d}\\log Q_{\\Lambda_L}(A_L) =-\\min\\{I(P):\\rho(P)\\in\\overline D,\\ \\Phi(P)\\leq e\\}.$$\nLet $R_{L,\\omega}$ be the periodized empirical field of [[def-tdmath-periodized-empirical-field]]. There are $\\beta\\geq0$ and $z>0$ such that the empirical fields under the conditional law $Q_{\\Lambda_L}(\\cdot\\mid A_L)$ and the matching periodic grand-canonical law concentrate in every open $\\tau_L$ neighborhood of the common minimizer set of $I+\\beta\\Phi-\\rho\\log z$ when $\\beta>0$. At $\\beta=0$, retain the forbidden-pair set $B=\\{x:\\phi(x)=+\\infty\\}$ and use $I-\\rho\\log z$ on the hard-admissible stationary class $\\mathcal K_B$, with grand weight $z^N\\mathbf1_{\\rm admissible}$; no value is assigned to $0\\cdot(+\\infty)$. If this minimizer is unique, both empirical fields converge to it in probability in $\\tau_L$. These are empirical-field conclusions. The periodically continued conditional and grand laws also converge in $\\tau_L$ under the same uniqueness assumption, for all measurable local observables of at-most-linear count growth. The energy constraint is an upper event and $D$ is nondegenerate; this does not assert exact $N$, a shrinking shell, or a singular energy surface.",
            "current_raw_sha256": "ada79f44ed00fe9f21bd05797f4addc19ec6b55b653174cc74e1712ecbc5541d",
            "scope_only_not_proof_acceptance": true
          }
        },
        {
          "case": "zero",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "Statement/Example scope: Assume the superstable regular model and Poisson conventions of [[thm-tdmath-continuum-variational-pressure]]. Case zero: step 4.1 addresses the zero case.",
