    "ex-tdmath-close-packing-endpoint": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-tdmath-superstable-regular-interaction",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "With fixed numerical length and energy units, the canonical positional partition is\n$$Z(L,N;\\beta)=\\frac1{N!}\\int_{\\Lambda_L^N}e^{-\\beta H_\\phi(q_1,\\ldots,q_N)}dq_1\\cdots dq_N,\\quad Z(L,0;\\beta)=1,$$\nwhere $\\beta>0$, and the grand partition is $\\Xi(L;a,\\beta)=\\sum_{N\\geq0}a^NZ(L,N;\\beta)$ for numerical activity $a>0$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "thm-tdmath-canonical-interior-density-limit",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "On the interior of $\\{\\rho:F(\\rho)>-\\infty\\}$, $F$ is finite and continuous.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "def-product-measure-on-sigma-finite-spaces",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "This is the\n**product measure** of $\\mu$ and $\\nu$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "thm-lebesgue-measure-of-a-box-of-every-kind",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Then $R^{\\circ}$ is open and\n$\\overline{R}$ is closed, so both are Borel and Lebesgue measurable, and **every**\nset $R$ with $R^{\\circ} \\subseteq R \\subseteq \\overline{R}$ is Lebesgue\nmeasurable with",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "thm-sigma-finite-product-measure-exists-is-rectangular-and-is-unique",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "1. the set function $E \\mapsto (\\mu \\times \\nu)(E)$ of\n   [[def-product-measure-on-sigma-finite-spaces]] is a measure on\n   $\\mathcal A \\otimes \\mathcal B$;\n2. for measurable rectangles,\n   $$(\\mu \\times \\nu)(A \\times B)=\\mu(A)\\nu(B),$$\n   with the nonnegative extended-product convention $0\\cdot\\infty=\\infty\\cdot0=0$;\n3. the measure $\\mu \\times \\nu$ is sigma-finite; and\n4. it is the unique measure on $\\mathcal A \\otimes \\mathcal B$ with the\n   rectangle formula.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "thm-tonelli-theorem-for-sigma-finite-product-spaces",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $(X,\\mathcal A,\\mu)$ and $(Y,\\mathcal B,\\nu)$ be sigma-finite measure\nspaces, and let $f : X \\times Y \\to [0,\\infty]$ be product-measurable.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "def-factorial-and-falling-factorial",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "**The factorial.** By the recursion theorem ([[thm-recursion]]) applied to the\nset $\\mathbb{N}\\times\\mathbb{N}$, the starting element $(0,1)$ and the function\n$f(k, v) = (\\sigma(k),\\, v \\cdot \\sigma(k))$, and by the same induction on the\nfirst coordinate as in [[def-nat-finite-sum-and-product]], there is a unique\n$F : \\mathbb{N} \\to \\mathbb{N}$ with",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "thm-lebesgue-outer-measure-and-measurability-are-translation-invariant",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "1. $\\lambda_n^*(E+h) = \\lambda_n^*(E)$ for **every** subset $E$;\n2. $E$ is Lebesgue measurable if and only if $E+h$ is;\n3. $\\lambda_n(E+h) = \\lambda_n(E)$ for every Lebesgue measurable $E$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "proof-step-1-1",
          "claim": "Order the coordinates $0\\leq x_1<\\cdots<x_N\\leq L$. The exclusion constraints are $x_{i+1}-x_i\\geq a$. Apply the coordinatewise translation $x_i\\mapsto y_i=x_i-(i-1)a$; each one-coordinate translation preserves Lebesgue measure [F5], so product measure is preserved. The ordered region becomes $0\\leq y_1\\leq\\cdots\\leq y_N\\leq S$, where $S=L-(N-1)a$. The cube $[0,S]^N$ splits, up to equality sets of zero product measure, into $N!$ coordinate-order regions. Product-measure uniqueness makes these permutation chambers equal in measure, so the ordered region has volume $S^N/N!$. The full labeled allowed volume is $N!$ times this, and canonical division by $N!$ gives $Z(L,N;\\beta)=S^N/N!$.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F5",
            "F1",
            "F3",
            "F4"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "proof-step-2-1",
          "claim": "If $L=(N-1)a$, then $S=0$ and the transformed region is a null set; its integral is zero. If $L>(N-1)a$, then $S>0$ and the ordered region has positive product volume. Thus the maximal-density endpoint does not carry a positive canonical partition at exact close packing; the interior-only theorem [F2] makes no contrary assertion.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F2",
            "F1",
            "F3",
            "F4",
            "step 1.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "routine_steps": [],
      "finite_checks": [
        {
          "name": "two-rod endpoint and interior volume",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "F3",
            "F4",
            "F5",
            "step 1.1",
            "step 2.1"
          ],
          "expected": "For a=1,N=2, L=1 gives S=0 and Z=0; L=2 gives S=1 and Z=1/2."
        }
      ],
      "boundaries": [
        {
          "case": "empty",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "In one dimension, let the pair potential be infinite when the separation is less than $a>0$, and zero otherwise. Boundary empty: The Statement has no empty-configuration branch; its claim concerns the displayed positive-volume, density, or conditional regime.",
          "case_scope": {
            "source_section": "Example",
            "source_excerpt": "In one dimension, let the pair potential be infinite when the separation is less than $a>0$, and zero otherwise. For $N\\geq1$ labeled rods in $[0,L]$, with canonical positional normalization $1/N!$, write $S=L-(N-1)a$. If $S>0$, translating the ordered coordinates by $y_i=x_i-(i-1)a$ gives\n$$Z(L,N;\\beta)=\\frac{S^N}{N!}=\\frac{(L-(N-1)a)^N}{N!},\\qquad \\beta>0.$$\nIf $S=0$, the allowed configuration set has zero Lebesgue measure and $Z(L,N;\\beta)=0$. Along $N\\to\\infty$ with $L=(N-1)a$, the density $N/L\\to1/a$ while every finite-volume partition is zero. Thus the thermodynamic close-packing endpoint is approached by a sequence of zero-weight finite systems; it is not realized by a positive canonical partition. The interior canonical limit theorem makes no assertion at that endpoint.",
            "current_raw_sha256": "dfb066527f5009e72ce1681a9ab1d7a4b209605648576900be94de511a699c10",
            "scope_only_not_proof_acceptance": true
          }
        },
