    "lem-tdmath-periodic-law-local-tame-transfer": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "A1",
          "source": "def-axiom-of-choice",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "The **Axiom of Choice** (AC) is the following statement.\n\n> Every family of nonempty sets has a choice function\n> ([[def-choice-function]]).\n\nWritten out: for every set $\\mathcal{F}$ all of whose members are nonempty,\nthere exists a function $g$ with domain $\\mathcal{F}$ satisfying $g(S) \\in S$ for\nall $S \\in \\mathcal{F}$.\n\nAn equivalent formulation is that a product of nonempty sets is nonempty: if\n$X_i \\ne \\emptyset$ for every $i \\in I$, then $\\prod_{i \\in I} X_i \\ne \\emptyset$.\nHere $\\prod_{i \\in I} X_i$ is the set of functions $f$ with domain $I$ such that\n$f(i) \\in X_i$ for every $i \\in I$; when a family of nonempty sets is indexed by\nitself, such an $f$ is precisely a choice function for it.",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "A1",
          "source": "def-ultrafilter-extension-principle",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "The **ultrafilter extension principle** says that every filter on a set is contained in an ultrafilter on that set ([[def-ultrafilter]]).\n\nIt is also called the **ultrafilter lemma** and is equivalent over ZF to the Boolean prime ideal theorem, abbreviated BPI. Items below write **assume UL/BPI** precisely when they use this extension principle; they do not thereby assume the full axiom of choice.",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "A1",
          "source": "def-dependent-choice",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $X$ be a set and let $R \\subseteq X \\times X$ be a binary relation on $X$.\nCall $R$ **entire on $X$** when\n\n$$\\text{for every } x \\in X \\text{ there is } y \\in X \\text{ with } x \\mathbin{R} y .$$\n\nThe **Axiom of Dependent Choice**, written $\\mathrm{DC}$, is the following\nstatement.\n\n> For every nonempty set $X$, every relation $R$ entire on $X$, and every\n> $a \\in X$, there is a function $x : \\mathbb{N} \\to X$ ([[def-function]],\n> [[def-natural-numbers]]) with\n> $$x_0 = a \\qquad \\text{and} \\qquad x_n \\mathbin{R} x_{n+1} \\ \\text{ for every } n \\in \\mathbb{N}.$$\n\nHere a sequence in $X$ means a function from $\\mathbb{N}$ to $X$, not necessarily\na real-valued sequence. As everywhere in this library $\\mathbb{N}$ contains $0$, and the sequence is\nindexed from $0$; the term $x_0$ is the prescribed starting point $a$ and every\nlater term is related to its predecessor.\n\n**What DC adds to what came before.** [[def-choice-function]] and\n[[def-axiom-of-choice]] select one element from each member of a family that is\nfixed in advance, and [[def-countable-choice]] does the same for a family indexed\nby $\\mathbb{N}$. In both, the family is given before any selection is made. DC is\nthe principle needed when the $n$-th set to select from is not known until the\nfirst $n$ selections have been made: here the admissible values of $x_{n+1}$ are\nexactly the $R$-successors of $x_n$, so the family being chosen from is built\nalong the choosing. Thus the displayed recursion is directly an instance of\nDC; it is not itself an application of the fixed-family formulation of\ncountable choice. This observation concerns the input to the two axioms and\nmakes no claim about a nonimplication between them.\n\n**The starting point may be dropped.** The formally weaker statement obtained by\ndeleting the clause $x_0 = a$ — for every nonempty $X$ and every entire $R$ there\nis a sequence with $x_n \\mathbin{R} x_{n+1}$ for all $n$ — is an immediate\nconsequence of the form above, since $X$ is nonempty and any of its elements may\nbe taken as $a$. Conversely, assume the start-free form, and fix $X$, $R$ and $a$ as above. Let $P$ be the set of all functions $p:k\\to X$ with nonzero $k\\in\\mathbb N$, $p(0)=a$, and $p(i)\\mathbin R p(i+1)$ whenever $i+1<k$. It is a set by Separation inside $\\mathcal P(\\mathbb N\\times X)$ ([[def-power-set]], [[def-cartesian-product]], [[def-axiom-schema-of-separation]]); a natural is the set of its predecessors ([[lem-nat-order-is-membership]]). The singleton path $\\{(0,a)\\}$ lies in $P$.\n\nOn $P$ let $p\\mathbin S q$ mean that $q$ extends $p$ by exactly one new last entry. This relation is entire: for one particular $p:k\\to X$, write $k=j+1$ ([[lem-nat-nonzero-is-successor]]); an $R$-successor $y$ of $p(j)$ exists, and $q=p\\cup\\{(k,y)\\}$ is the required extension. This is existential instantiation for that path, not a simultaneous choice of successors. Start-free DC now supplies a sequence $(p_n)$ with $p_n\\mathbin S p_{n+1}$; its first path need not be the singleton path, but every path in $P$ already starts at $a$.\n\nTheir graphs are nested. By induction ([[thm-induction-principle]]), $p_n$ has at least $n+1$ entries: the initial path is nonempty, and every step adds one entry. Hence $x=\\bigcup_{n\\in\\mathbb N}p_n$ is a function with domain $\\mathbb N$ and values in $X$. Indeed, nestedness makes all common values agree, every natural index eventually lies in a path, and no path has indices outside $\\mathbb N$. Each path starts at $a$, so $x(0)=a$; for any $i$, a sufficiently long path contains both $i$ and $i+1$, giving $x(i)\\mathbin R x(i+1)$. Replacement and Union form this union of the sequence of graphs. Thus the start-free and prescribed-start forms are equivalent in ZF. Every use below retains the prescribed-start convention.\n\n**$R$ need not be an order and the terms need not be distinct.** What DC delivers\nis a sequence, that is a function $\\mathbb{N} \\to X$, not a chain in the\norder-theoretic sense ([[def-chain]]). The relation may be symmetric, and the\nsequence may repeat a value or be constant; all that is asserted is\n$x_n \\mathbin{R} x_{n+1}$ at every index.",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "A1",
          "source": "def-hahn-banach-extension-principle-relative",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Work over ZF. The **real dominated-extension principle**, denoted **HB**, is the following assertion:\n\nFor every real vector space $X$, every sublinear functional $p:X\\to\\mathbb R$ in the sense of [[def-sublinear-functional]], every real linear subspace $M\\le X$ in the sense of [[def-linear-subspace]], and every real linear functional $g:M\\to\\mathbb R$ in the sense of [[def-algebraic-dual-and-linear-functional]],\n$$\\bigl(\\forall m\\in M,\\ g(m)\\le p(m)\\bigr)\\ \\Longrightarrow\\ \\exists F:X\\to\\mathbb R\\ \\bigl(F\\text{ is real linear},\\ F|_M=g,\\ \\forall x\\in X,\\ F(x)\\le p(x)\\bigr).$$\n\nThis names an additional principle; it does not assert a proof of HB in ZF. Subsequent results explicitly state when they assume HB. Neither topology nor completeness is part of this assertion. The subspace may be $\\{0\\}$ or all of $X$. Sublinearity at scalar zero gives $p(0)=0$, and a linear functional has value zero at zero.",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-tdmath-periodized-empirical-field",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Use the cube $\\Lambda_L=[0,L)^d$ and the translation $T_x$ defined in [[def-tdmath-poisson-configuration-reference]]. For a finite configuration $\\omega\\subset\\Lambda_L$, let $\\omega^{(L)}=\\{q+Lk:q\\in\\omega,\\ k\\in\\mathbb Z^d\\}$ be its periodic continuation. Define its **periodized empirical field**\n$$R_{L,\\omega}:=\\frac1{L^d}\\int_{\\Lambda_L}\\delta_{T_x\\omega^{(L)}}\\,dx.$$\nThe integral is the push-forward of normalized Lebesgue measure on $\\Lambda_L$ under $x\\mapsto T_x\\omega^{(L)}$. For a probability law $C_L$ on finite configurations in $\\Lambda_L$, define its **empirical-field barycenter** $\\bar C_L$ by\n$$\\bar C_L(f):=\\int C_L(d\\omega)\\,R_{L,\\omega}(f)=\\frac1{L^d}\\int_{\\Lambda_L}E_{C_L}[f(T_x\\omega^{(L)})]\\,dx$$\nfor local tame $f$ from [[def-tdmath-local-tame-topology]]. This is a spatial average over the finite box, not the raw finite-volume law viewed at a fixed origin.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "lem-tdmath-periodized-empirical-field-stationary-density",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "For $L>0$ and a finite configuration $\\omega\\subset\\Lambda_L$ with $N(\\omega)=N$, the measure $R_{L,\\omega}$ of [[def-tdmath-periodized-empirical-field]] is a stationary probability law of finite intensity and\n$$\\rho(R_{L,\\omega})=\\frac{N}{L^d}.$$\nFor any probability law $C_L$ on finite configurations in $\\Lambda_L$ with $E_{C_L}N<\\infty$, its empirical-field barycenter $\\bar C_L$ is also a stationary probability law of finite intensity and $\\rho(\\bar C_L)=E_{C_L}N/L^d$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "lem-tdmath-relative-entropy-variational-and-chain-rules",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume AC. Let $P,Q$ be probability measures on one measurable space and use $D(P\\Vert Q)$ as defined in [[def-tdmath-finite-region-relative-entropy]]. Then\n$$D(P\\Vert Q)=\\sup_{\\phi\\in B_b}\\left(P(\\phi)-\\log Q(e^\\phi)\\right),$$\nwhere $B_b$ is the set of bounded real measurable functions. In particular $D\\geq0$. If $T$ is measurable, then $D(P\\circ T^{-1}\\Vert Q\\circ T^{-1})\\leq D(P\\Vert Q)$.\n\nFor standard-Borel spaces $X,Y$, probability reference measures $Q_X,Q_Y$, and a joint probability $P_{XY}\\ll Q_X\\otimes Q_Y$ with finite $D(P_{XY}\\Vert Q_X\\otimes Q_Y)$, let $P_{Y|X=x}$ be the conditional law supplied by [[thm-conditional-density-formula]]. Then\n$$D(P_{XY}\\Vert Q_X\\otimes Q_Y)=D(P_X\\Vert Q_X)+\\int_X D(P_{Y|X=x}\\Vert Q_Y)\\,P_X(dx).$$\nMore generally, for nested standard-Borel observations $R$ and $S$ with $R$ a measurable function of $S$, and a fixed probability reference $Q_Y$ for a further variable $Y$, the conditional increments\n$$G(R):=E_P\\!\\left[D(P_{Y|R}\\Vert Q_Y)\\right],\\qquad G(S):=E_P\\!\\left[D(P_{Y|S}\\Vert Q_Y)\\right]$$\nsatisfy $0\\leq G(R)\\leq G(S)$ whenever the joint relative entropy is finite.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "thm-tdmath-poisson-reference-count-properties",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume AC, fix $d\\geq1$ and $\\lambda>0$, and let $Q_\\lambda$ be the configuration law of [[def-tdmath-poisson-configuration-reference]]. For pairwise disjoint bounded Borel sets $A_1,\\ldots,A_r\\subseteq\\mathbb R^d$, the restrictions $\\omega|_{A_1},\\ldots,\\omega|_{A_r}$ are independent, each count $N_{A_j}$ has Poisson law with mean $\\lambda|A_j|$, and the joint law of counts on any finite disjoint family is the product of those Poisson laws. In particular, $Q_\\lambda$ is invariant under every translation $T_x$, and its intensity measure is $\\lambda$ times Lebesgue measure.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "thm-tdmath-local-tame-entropy-compactness",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume AC, the ultrafilter extension principle, DC, and HB. Let\n$$\\mathcal H_c:=\\{P\\in\\mathcal P^1_{\\rm stat}(\\Omega): I(P)\\leq c\\},\\qquad c\\in[0,\\infty),$$\nwhere $I$ and the local-tame topology $\\tau_L$ are defined by [[thm-tdmath-specific-entropy-density]] and [[def-tdmath-local-tame-topology]]. Then $\\mathcal H_c$ is compact and sequentially compact in $\\tau_L$. This is a pure measure-theoretic compactness theorem for simple locally finite configurations relative to the independent unit-intensity Poisson reference; it makes no thermodynamic-limit or physical claim.",
          "uses": [
            "3.1",
            "4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "thm-tdmath-specific-entropy-density",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume AC and let $P\\in\\mathcal P^1_{\\rm stat}(\\Omega)$, with this class defined in [[def-tdmath-local-tame-topology]], be stationary for the translations of [[def-tdmath-poisson-configuration-reference]]. For a bounded Borel set $G$, write $P_G$ and $Q_G$ for the restrictions of $P$ and the unit-intensity Poisson reference $Q$ to configurations in $G$. For $L>0$, put $\\Lambda_L=[0,L)^d$ and\n$$D_L:=D(P_{\\Lambda_L}\\Vert Q_{\\Lambda_L}).$$\nThen the specific relative entropy density\n$$I(P):=\\lim_{L\\to\\infty}\\frac{D_L}{L^d}$$\nexists in $[0,+\\infty]$ and satisfies\n$$I(P)=\\sup_{m\\in\\mathbb N,\\ m\\geq1}\\frac{D(P_{\\Lambda_m}\\Vert Q_{\\Lambda_m})}{m^d}.$$\nIf $I(P)<\\infty$, every bounded Borel restriction has finite relative entropy. This is an information functional relative to the specified unit-intensity Poisson law; changing the reference changes its value.",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F6",
          "source": "thm-monotone-convergence-for-the-integral",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $0\\le f_1\\le f_2\\le\\cdots$ be measurable and suppose $f_n(x)\\uparrow f(x)$\nfor every $x$. Then\n$$\\int f_n\\,d\\mu\\uparrow\\int f\\,d\\mu.$$",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F6",
          "source": "thm-dominated-convergence",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $f$ and $(f_n)$ be measurable complex-valued functions such that\n$f_n\\to f$ almost everywhere and $|f_n|\\le g$ almost everywhere for a single\nnonnegative measurable function $g$ with $\\int g\\,d\\mu<+\\infty$. Then\n$f\\in L^1(\\mu)$,\n$$\\int|f_n-f|\\,d\\mu\\longrightarrow0,$$\nand hence\n$$\\int f_n\\,d\\mu\\longrightarrow\\int f\\,d\\mu.$$",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "proof-step-1-1",
          "claim": "For bounded local $f$, torus invariance gives $E_{C_L}f(T_x\\omega^{(L)})=E_{C_L}f(\\omega^{(L)})$ for every $x$. Integrating over $\\Lambda_L$ proves $\\widetilde C_L(f)=E_{C_L}R_{L,\\omega}(f)$. In particular the continued law is stationary. The entropy inequality with bounded truncations of $aN_{\\Lambda_L}$ and [F3] gives finite $E_{C_L}N$, so the intensity assertion of [F1] applies.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F3",
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "F6"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "proof-step-2-1",
          "claim": "Fix an integer $a\\geq1$ and take $L>a$. The fundamental cube contains $m_L=\\lfloor L/a\\rfloor^d$ disjoint translates of $W=",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F2",
            "step 1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "proof-step-3-1",
          "claim": "On a bounded $A\\subset W$, apply the entropy inequality to $t\\min(N_A\\mathbf1_{\\{N_A>K\\}},M)$ and let $M\\to\\infty$. If $C_W$ is the uniform bound from step 2.1, then\n$$tE_{\\widetilde C_L}[N_A\\mathbf1_{\\{N_A>K\\}}]\\leq C_W+\\log E_{Q_W}e^{tN_A\\mathbf1_{\\{N_A>K\\}}}.$$\nFor fixed $t>0$, the logarithm tends to zero as $K\\to\\infty$ by [F3] and [F6]. First take $t$ large and then $K$ large. This proves uniform count-tail integrability. The local weak-density compactness and consistent marginal extension in [F4] now give subsequential convergence for bounded local tests; the uniform tails upgrade it to $\\tau_L$ convergence. Every limit is stationary. Lower semicontinuity of finite-region entropy and step 2.1 imply $a^{-d}D(P_W\\Vert Q_W)\\leq\\liminf c_L$ for every integer $a$ along a subsequence realizing the liminf; [F5] proves the stated entropy bound.",
          "step": "3.1",
          "inputs": [
            "step 2.1",
            "F3",
            "F6",
            "F4",
            "F5",
            "A1",
            "F2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "proof-step-4-1",
          "claim": "If the empirical fields converge in probability to $P_*$, then for bounded local $f$ the bounded random variables $R_{L,\\omega}(f)$ converge in probability to $P_*(f)$, hence their expectations converge. By step 1.1 this is convergence of $\\widetilde C_L(f)$. For tame $f$, truncate on $\\{N_A\\leq K\\}$; step 3.1 makes the truncation error uniformly small, and the same is true for the limit law. Taking $L\\to\\infty$ and then $K\\to\\infty$ proves convergence for every stated tame observable.",
          "step": "4.1",
          "inputs": [
            "step 1.1",
            "step 3.1",
            "F4"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "routine_steps": [],
      "boundaries": [
        {
          "case": "empty",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "Statement permits the zero-particle configuration but requires each C_L to be a probability law. A deterministic empty law has finite entropy density and the empty periodic continuation."
        },
        {
          "case": "zero",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "Statement entropy densities may be zero; the local marginal bounds and step 3.1 count tails require no division by entropy. L>a and positive a,t ensure the actual denominators are nonzero."
        },
        {
          "case": "one",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "Statement dimension d=1 is included; the packing number is floor(L/a) and the same one-dimensional cube argument applies in step 2.1."
        },
        {
          "case": "degenerate",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "A point-mass limiting law is allowed, but a zero-volume torus is outside the L-to-infinity regime; entropy control concerns genuine finite Poisson reference volumes."
